/* Generated by campaign/build/build_docs.py from campaign/docs/*.html - do not edit by hand. */
window.WopDocs = {"chronicle": "<div class=\"document\">\n  <div class=\"doc-inner\">\n\n\n    <div class=\"page-eyebrow\">Compiled from the session audio · most recent first</div>\n    <h1 class=\"page-title\">The Chronicle</h1>\n    <p class=\"page-subtitle\">A running record of the envoy's nights</p>\n    <div class=\"tag-legend\">\n      <span><span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Coterie</span> player character</span>\n      <span><span class=\"char-tag npc\">NPC</span> non-player character</span>\n      <span><span class=\"char-tag extra\">Others</span> retainers, familiars &amp; walk-ons</span>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- ══════════ SESSION FOUR ══════════ -->\n    <div class=\"session-block\">\n      <div class=\"session-masthead\">\n        <div class=\"sm-left\">\n          <span class=\"session-num\">Session Four</span>\n          <span class=\"session-name\">Searching for the Kill</span>\n        </div>\n        <span class=\"session-date\">The wood beyond Shrewsbury · 1242</span>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"session-standfirst\">The Earl got his hunt at last — into the wood the Roman roads refuse, after a stag that will not stay killed. What waited there was worse than a stag: a fae driven out of its own trees, a family of poachers used as bait, a dead houndmaster who turns other men's weapons against his master, and, under all of it, a black pool the forest rots around. Someone died who did not have to. He was one of their own.</p>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">I.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">Setting Out</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">The Earl's own hunting-train had thinned — the last pursuit of the stag had cost him dogs, and men, and a houndmaster — so it was <strong>Tomisława</strong>'s people who filled the gaps: the <strong>Graf</strong> to hunt in earnest, <strong>Andrzej</strong> on the trail, <strong>Piers</strong> on the bow. <strong>Oscar</strong> rode the front, half-racing <strong>Blackwell</strong> for it; Tomisława kept to the rear, telling herself she had come only to watch — because she suspects the stag and the thing Mithras sent them for are the same thing. Andrzej is very good. He and Oscar cut the trail almost at once, and cut it clean, and the Earl was frankly impressed by the quality of a tracker who was not his own.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">the Graf</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Andrzej</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Piers</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">II.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">War Stories</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">With the tracking in better hands than his own, <strong>Blackwell</strong> did what he does — called for war stories to pass the miles. <strong>Oscar</strong> would not give one; the <strong>Graf</strong> would not either, and the silence of both men sat heavy on the ride. <strong>Eustace</strong> filled it, and filled it well: embellished tales of the Barons' Uprising, of carrying the prince's secrets through a war he was too small to be suspected in, turning even a child's face into a blade for Mithras. The Earl laughed till his belly shook. Behind them, the Graf's face had gone somewhere else entirely; he began to mutter in Old Polish, low, and did not appear to know that he was doing it.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">The war-talk was a diversion to the Earl and a door to the Graf. Something walked out of it behind his eyes and did not come back for a long while.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">the Graf</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">III.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">Tools and Conquerors</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\"><strong>Dylan</strong> rode up alongside <strong>Oscar</strong>, and the talk turned to what men like them are for — tools, weapons, taken up by princes and set down again; how Britain is only the newest name laid over Roman and Saxon and Viking and French, conqueror atop conqueror until no one remembers who they were. The <strong>Graf</strong> spoke into it at last, and it was worse than his silence: called up by the <strong>Duke of Silesia</strong> to hold the eastern marches against the <strong>Mongols</strong>, he had watched his villages burned, his people driven ahead of the horde as living shields, his lords' heads taken and their ears counted into bags. <em>I am a lord in name only,</em> he said, <em>with no people and no land</em> — and he hopes, without understanding her ways, that his bride will one night carry them back East with strength enough to matter. Oscar took it in with his own history behind his teeth; and Dylan, quietly, told Oscar he saw something good in him still, and that a man who refuses to lead may only hurt more people by the refusing.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Dylan</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">the Graf</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">IV.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Boy in the Wood</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">At a pause in the tracking, while the others bent to the dirt, <strong>Méabh</strong> and <strong>Eustace</strong> alone heard it — a small voice off in the trees, calling <em>boy</em>. No one else turned a head. They followed an ember, a little light that kindled and guttered and kindled again, to a thing resting on a branch: a horned beetle no bigger than a thumb, its shell catching a glow that had no source, and, on the underside of it, a small human face — beady glowing eyes, a wet little mouth working in its belly. It called them <em>cousin</em>. It knew <strong>Méabh</strong>, or knew something that had worn her shape a long while ago, before a great cat chased it off. The fair folk do not hold this wood any more, it told them; something darker took it, and the stag they hunt is <em>nothing magical</em>. It was hungry. It would lead them to the bad thing in the trees — if first they fed it something that hurt.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">Eustace has never fit among the Kindred half so easily as he fit, in that moment, with the wicked little thing that crawled into his pocket.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">the fae</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">V.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Poacher Camp</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\"><strong>Andrzej</strong> turned up two trails at once — the quarry's, and poachers camped in the Earl's wood. Blackwell would have dealt them justice, but it meant delay; <strong>Eustace</strong> offered to see to it, and drew <strong>Méabh</strong> and the beetle off into the dark. What followed was the ugliest hour of the night. Eustace went in as a lost, weeping child — <em>a bad man did bad things to me</em> — and once they had gathered him in close, turned the camp on itself: a father and his sons and a boy made to strike one another, mouth and fist, while the beetle drank their terror and Eustace drank his fill. <strong>Méabh</strong> woke the plants of the camp with blood and poppy and set them writhing up out of the ground to hold the men where they stood.</p>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">There was a father who would not run — who put his own body and his own fear between the vines and his half-grown son — and something in <strong>Méabh</strong> let the boy go: she held her own plants back by main will while the two of them fled into the trees, and did not follow. The rest she left for the roots to feed on. When it was done, the beetle was fat and content, and led them on, deeper.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">They played the cruellest of tricks on four men who will never trust one another again — and fed a fae on it, and called it a night's work.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">the fae</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">VI.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Hounds Again</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Deep in now, <strong>Tomisława</strong> heard them first — the low, wrong belling she had heard once before, on the road to Shrewsbury. She knows enough of the East to fear werewolves, and knew at once that these were not that, which was its own mercy: against werewolves she was unready to face, she would simply have died. But they were no natural hounds either — a hunting-cry with an ethereal echo laid under it, a cold that walks up the spine of its own accord. Quietly she drew the <strong>Graf</strong> back and told him to help her bring the hunt to a close before whatever owned that sound arrived to meet them.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">the Graf</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">VII.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Houndmaster</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">It arrived. A weather-beaten huntsman stepped out of the air itself — a broken horn at his neck, a spear in his hand, his eyes fixed on <strong>Blackwell</strong> and nothing else. <strong>John Rafe</strong>, the Earl's own houndmaster, dead in the last pursuit of this same stag; Blackwell's pride and temper had cost the man his dogs and then his life. <em>Should have been your head off in these woods,</em> he told the Earl. <em>I would rather you had killed me than made me watch you butcher them.</em> He could not lay a hand on the living, and did not need to: <strong>Oscar</strong>'s sword tore out of its scabbard toward the Earl's neck, and Oscar caught it a hair short; <strong>Gareth</strong>'s spear twisted in his grip; the wood filled with black hounds that came apart into smoke where they were struck, and bit like anything living until they did. <strong>Tomisława</strong> read the shape of it — a spirit of vengeance bound to the Earl — and told him plainly that he might be the one who has to answer for it. <em>We can kill it,</em> Blackwell said, which was not an answer. She opened her wrist over the earth and began the long ritual that listens for where power runs.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">John Rafe</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Gareth</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">VIII.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">What Oscar Did</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">The dead man found the one blade that would do the most harm. He took <strong>Oscar</strong>'s sword, and Oscar's own worst horror with it — a weapon in his hand turned against his own — and drove them together. The blow that landed on <strong>Blackwell</strong> would have taken a mortal head clean from its shoulders; the Earl is a hard old Ventrue, and kept his, barely. It was enough. <strong>John Rafe</strong> breathed out, satisfied, and was gone. But Oscar was gone too, into a full hunger frenzy — and <strong>Blackwell</strong>, bleeding and unbothered, simply reached into the Beast with Dominate and pointed it at the nearest of Tomisława's people: <strong>Piers</strong>. <strong>Tomisława</strong> compelled the <strong>Graf</strong> to <em>hold</em>, put her horse between <strong>Gareth</strong> and the thing about to happen so the boy would not throw himself into it, and — coldly, because the alternative was losing one of her own to save a man who was barely hers — did nothing else. She told <strong>Dylan</strong> to do as he saw fit. Oscar bore Piers to the ground, tore his throat open, and drank him down to nothing while the Earl looked on and grinned. <em>That's my boy.</em></p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">Not an enemy. Not a stranger. One of their own, dead for no reason but that a dead man and a bored lord had between them found the crack in Oscar and leaned on it.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">John Rafe</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Piers</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">the Graf</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Gareth</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Dylan</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">IX.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">After the Kill</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\"><strong>Oscar</strong> came back to himself holding a drained body, and understood what it was, and dropped it, and ran — nowhere in particular, only away. <strong>Blackwell</strong>, who had felt nothing pass but sport, took it for a dash toward the stag and gave cheerful chase; and when he caught him, Oscar could barely stand upright under it, and the Earl could not begin to understand it. <em>These things happen,</em> he said. <em>It's the merciful ones you have to watch.</em> <strong>Tomisława</strong> left the burying of Piers to the <strong>Graf</strong> and <strong>Andrzej</strong> and finished her listening: there is a furcus in this wood, a convergence of real power — and, so far as she can tell, it has nothing to do with whatever else rots here. <strong>Dylan</strong> stayed at her side and asked her plainly what she truly seeks. <em>The power to return to my home,</em> she told him, which was true.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Dylan</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">the Graf</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Andrzej</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">X.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Black Pool</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">The beetle led <strong>Méabh</strong> and <strong>Eustace</strong> to it at last: a pool perhaps ten feet across, the water in it gone thick and black as tar, set among the trees like a wound that would not close. <strong>Méabh</strong> felt the rot of it through every root she is joined to; <strong>Eustace</strong> cast his shadow down into it and felt the abyss turn over and look back up at him. This, the beetle said, is the bad thing — what drove the fair folk out of their own wood — and, fed and content, it took its leave. But not before Méabh drew out of it that the <em>self</em> it remembered wearing her face was a long, long time ago, and quietly pocketed the name of a wood she now means to go and find.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">Whatever the Earl believes he has been hunting, it lies downstream of this: a black eye open in the earth, and something behind it that noticed Eustace notice it.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">the fae</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">XI.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Thing with Antlers</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">They hid — shadow drawn close about them, <strong>Méabh</strong> and <strong>Scéalán</strong> gone perfectly still — as the stag came through. It was vast, and black to the hide as if it had been dipped in the pool itself, its eyes a dull, banked red; old gashes crossed it everywhere, dried and long bloodless, wounds that should each have killed it and plainly had not; and its antlers moved on their own, jagged, writhing, reaching out into the dark as though hungry for something. It put its great nose down to the very spot where they lay hidden — and then broke, and ran. What it is — a beast at all, or only the shape of one — not one of them could say. <strong>Blackwell</strong> and <strong>Oscar</strong> were closing in from one side, <strong>Dylan</strong>'s head coming up at the sound of it, when the night drew its breath and held. The kill is still out there. So is everyone hunting it.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Scéalán</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Dylan</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- THREADS -->\n      <div class=\"section\" style=\"margin-top:2rem;\">\n        <div class=\"section-rule\">\n          <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n          <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n          <h2 class=\"section-title\">Threads Left Hanging</h2>\n          <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n          <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        </div>\n        <ul class=\"thread-list\">\n          <li><strong>The black pool.</strong> A tar-dark corruption deep in the wood that drove out the fair folk. Méabh feels it in the roots; Eustace felt the abyss answer his shadow. What it is — and whether it made the Earl's stag — is unknown.</li>\n          <li><strong>The stag.</strong> Vast, black, blood-eyed, hung with wounds that never took, its antlers moving on their own. Whether it is even an animal, no one can say. Blackwell wants its head; Oscar has wagered him for it.</li>\n          <li><strong>A separate power.</strong> Tomisława's Seeking Tiamat found a true furcus in this wood — a convergence of power that, so far as she can tell, has nothing to do with the corruption. Two mysteries in one forest.</li>\n          <li><strong>Piers is dead.</strong> Killed by Oscar — frenzied by the ghost, then Dominated onto him by Blackwell. One of their own, for nothing. Oscar is not the same, and the Earl never noticed.</li>\n          <li><strong>John Rafe, sated for now.</strong> The houndmaster's ghost took its due — the Earl's blood, and a death — and faded. Whether it is laid to rest or only fed, no one knows; Blackwell means to \"kill it\" if it comes again.</li>\n          <li><strong>The fae's memory of Méabh.</strong> The beetle knew a wearer of her face from long ago, and named a wood. Méabh is hunting that name now.</li>\n          <li><strong>Oscar and the Beast.</strong> His deepest horror is a weapon turned against his own — and a dead man and a bored lord found it in a single night. He can kill the people he loves, and now he knows it.</li>\n        </ul>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- ══════════ SESSION THREE ══════════ -->\n    <div class=\"session-block\">\n      <div class=\"session-masthead\">\n        <div class=\"sm-left\">\n          <span class=\"session-num\">Session Three</span>\n          <span class=\"session-name\">Brother Larkin and the Bones of Saint Winifred</span>\n        </div>\n        <span class=\"session-date\">Shrewsbury · 1242</span>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"session-standfirst\">Two nights of grace before the hunt — a lord's apology, a mentor's leash set down, a mortal mother's frank counsel — and then a walk across the river to the abbey, where a genuinely devout monk, a saint's inert bones, and everyone's worst instincts turned a simple errand into a small catastrophe nobody had ordered.</p>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">I.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Drunkard's Vein</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Rising to a court laid out for them, the coterie took the vessels their host offered rather than hunt — <strong>Méabh</strong> on the principle that a smart predator eats what it is given, <strong>Tomisława</strong> working lightly off her own well-fed herd. It was <strong>Oscar</strong> who made the one particular request, to <strong>Blackwell</strong> himself: a <em>drunk</em> mortal to drink from. A lesser lord might have bristled at a guest dictating the menu; this one — a bored warrior-Ventrue rattling around his own keep for want of sport — found it an interesting choice, and went to arrange it personally.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">II.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Lord's Apology</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\"><strong>Eustace</strong> asked the Earl for a private word and got it — a quiet corner of a loud hall. He thanked Blackwell for his hospitality; Blackwell, unprompted, went further than courtesy required. <em>You will have to forgive Gareth — he is quite new to all this. While I felt correct in it, I should have been more gracious with you.</em> From there it warmed into something like rapport. Eustace, Embraced as a child and touchy about it, reframed the wound as an edge — <em>a different vantage</em>, an ease of moving through a world that underestimates him — and let slip, with some pride, that he is of genuine noble blood and never wanted for anything in mortal life. Blackwell knew the family, and said so.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">The tiff of the first night, closed — and the lord who had been made to bow now half an ally.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">III.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">In His Cups, and the Leash Set Down</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">The peace lasted until <strong>Oscar</strong> arrived — cheerfully, catastrophically drunk, having gone too deep on a drunkard of his own. He lurched up to the Earl (<em>are you still talking to this little fiend?</em>), forgot Blackwell's name twice, and nearly announced, in the middle of the man's own court, exactly what the envoy had really come to Shrewsbury to do. <strong>Eustace</strong> caught his eyes and mesmerized the worst of it back down — then thought better of throwing a discipline around in another lord's hall, where it is no small taboo and this Ventrue would surely have known it for what it was. He smoothed it over aloud as nothing but an apology for the previous night, and kept the real conversation for later.</p>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Later came in an alley off the hall. Eustace weighed simply Dominating the lesson in — Oscar would never know it had happened — and chose, for once, not to. He set the leash down and spoke to the man plainly: <em>you cannot tear me down in front of others, especially those of status.</em> Oscar, mellow and genuinely contrite under the drink, agreed. An understanding Eustace did not have to compel — which, for Eustace, sits close to tenderness.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">IV.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">Two of a Kind</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\"><strong>Lady Henrietta</strong> offered to take <strong>Tomisława</strong> and her household across to the abbey that evening, and the two women fell into the frankest talk either had had in the keep. Henrietta is a mortal mother watching her son groomed into a monster, kept — for now — by Blackwell's unusual leave to retain her own agency rather than be reduced to a servant or discarded. Tomisława answered in kind: her sire had marked her as a candidate, arranged her marriage to the <strong>Graf</strong> together with her parents, and turned her — binding the two of them as a mutual surety. <em>The marriage is legal, as long as one looks not too closely,</em> she said; a functional thing that has kept them both alive. Of all her sire did to her, she told Henrietta, it is <em>one of a small handful of things I do not resent.</em></p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Henrietta</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">the Graf</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">V.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Abbey Across the Water</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">The abbey stood beyond the keep — across the river that divides castle from town, a large, oppressive pile lately grown rich on pilgrims. <strong>Tomisława</strong> drew down her veils for the walk: three layered, near-crocheted panels over her face, because a shrine is full of crosses and she cannot look upon them. A monk at the door knew Henrietta, admitted the party as pilgrims come to see the relics, and went to rouse the one who keeps them. Henrietta made her introductions and withdrew — <em>I am not one of you</em> — leaving the coterie to it.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Henrietta</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">the Graf</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">VI.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">Brother Larkin</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\"><strong>Brother Larkin</strong> came out and simply <em>stared</em> — a long, uncomfortable beat at each of them in turn, and a frown he did not trouble to hide. A Cainite monk, and the rarest sort: genuinely, unshakeably devout. He revered <strong>Mary, the Sister of Sorrows</strong> — the Salubri nun from the beach — very nearly as a saint herself; spoke warmly of the <strong>Order of Absolution</strong> he has been begging Blackwell to garrison here; and admitted he had written even to <em>as-yet-unredeemed Tremere</em>, in hope of their souls. His theology was sincere and strange: God does not curse, and the Embrace is not damnation but a purpose still to be discovered. <strong>Eustace</strong>, who has no patience for sermons, baited him — <em>a loving God, who did this to me?</em> — and got, for his trouble, <em>watch your words in the house of God. You may leave.</em></p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Brother Larkin</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">VII.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Beast in the Nave</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">It was <strong>Méabh</strong> he could not let be. He named a <em>darkness</em> in her, something unnatural even by their lights, and moved close as if to correct it — and the room's Beast came up all at once. <strong>Oscar</strong> got a fist in the monk's robes and dragged him in, a voice not quite his own repeating <em>I don't know what you are talking about</em>; Méabh, worse, seized him too before she could stop herself. <strong>Tomisława</strong> ended it the only way left — lifting her veil past the crucifix he wore, spending will to force the words through the pain, and <em>compelling</em> him: <em>you will not harm her.</em> The command held. Méabh, near her own fight-or-flight, was sent out to Eustace; Tomisława and Oscar walked the shaking monk deeper in, toward the relic.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">A room full of predators, and the only real threat in it a lonely monk who saw too clearly what Méabh is.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Brother Larkin</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">VIII.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Bones That Weren't</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">The reliquary held a statue of <strong>Saint Winifred</strong> above a basin of still water, and under a pane of glass at its bottom, on red velvet, a skull and a scatter of bones. <strong>Tomisława</strong> read them the only way she can — a long, reverent-looking study that was in fact a koldun's appraisal — and found nothing. No aura, no engine, no residue of power: well-kept saint's bones and not one thing more. Whatever artifact Mithras sent them for, it was not here.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Brother Larkin</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">IX.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">A Mind Unmade</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\"><strong>Larkin</strong> could not abide her indifference to the holy — and, sincerely, without malice, reached into her mind. His clear, patient words punched through a haze she only half-noticed forming; the unravelling turned her round until she <em>believed</em> the bones mattered, and mattered enough to carry to the Order. She ordered <strong>Oscar</strong> to take them. He went for the glass — and the devout little monk drew a <em>stake</em> from his robes and put it into Oscar's back, missing the heart. What followed flattered no one: Tomisława reached for the Fleshcrafting to turn the monk's own body against him and found she has no such power — her Vicissitude reshapes only her own flesh; Oscar, staked, grappled the monk and lost his grip twice as the Beast's horror worked against him; at the last she <em>compelled</em> Larkin to feed, and the monk latched onto her arm, took a mouthful of her blood, and was finally pinned. Her head cleared as the compulsion faded. The bones, she decided, were not necessary after all.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">He had been breaking her mind; she broke his instead — over bones neither of them needed, which stayed exactly where they had always lain.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Brother Larkin</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">X.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">Parting in Shame</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">There was nothing to do but agree it had never happened. <strong>Tomisława</strong> promised the Order would hear <em>neither good nor ill</em> of his relics — <em>one such as you should not speak of them to anyone at all</em> — and <strong>Larkin</strong>, ashen and ashamed, led them out through his beautiful renovated nave, warning that <em>a ghost walks his halls</em> and that the night was no hour to wander. To <strong>Oscar</strong> he said the truest thing anyone managed all evening: that something weighs heavy on him, that this company will not ease it, and that he might seek another path. Oscar, half-drunk and long used to penance, took it without argument.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Brother Larkin</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">XI.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">Stab It</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Out front, where they had waited out the disaster, <strong>Eustace</strong> and <strong>Méabh</strong> played their game again. She admitted, in her sidelong way, what she hungers for — <em>safety</em>, where he hungers for power — and he admitted, to his own visible irritation, that he has grown <em>oddly fond</em> of her. They struck a compact and a private word for it, half a rabbit and half a stoat: if anyone ever tries to look under her dress, she has only to say <em>stab it</em>, and he will see them walk into a ditch. She patted him on the head, and gave him the barest true thing she could — and not one word past it.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">Two clever creatures, each certain the other is playing, and neither sure whether that is the reason to trust or the reason not to.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Scéalán</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">XII.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">A Home, Maybe</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Back at the keep <strong>Méabh</strong> sat down beside a drunk, sunk <strong>Oscar</strong> — sent by Eustace to draw him out, and finding she meant it. He gave her more of himself than he intended: a knight who broke faith with his own men to spare others and was cast out for it by his king, still paying the price and half-certain he deserves to. She gave back the one story she guards least — how she found <strong>Scéalán</strong> in the forest, a wolf abandoned with a broken leg, and carried him home, and fed him her own blood. <em>Why?</em> <em>Because he was alone.</em> <em>Not anymore.</em> They traded small questions into the night — and <strong>Dylan</strong> drifted close to listen, until Oscar caught him at it and Méabh flashed a fang, and the Welshman moved politely along.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Dylan</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Scéalán</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">XIII.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Stag, and the Wager</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Walking back, the coterie argued the divine — whether their kind can be pure of heart, whether God is a real power or merely a leash for the masses (Tomisława's view: real power, but shut to the likes of them). And the likelier answer to their errand had surfaced at the abbey by elimination: if the relic is not Winifred's bones, it is almost certainly the <strong>stag</strong> — a beast beheaded and restored again and again, mirroring the saint's own death and resurrection exactly. So the hunt <strong>Blackwell</strong> has been itching for became the mission. <strong>Oscar</strong> wagered the Earl for it — whoever fells the stag keeps it — and the two grinned at each other like the soldiers they both are. The <strong>Graf</strong> will hunt in earnest with <strong>Andrzej</strong> and <strong>Piers</strong> the bowman; Tomisława will follow to watch the woods; Eustace will ride with Méabh and the wolves. They broke for the day with the hunt set for the next night.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Dylan</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">the Graf</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- THREADS -->\n      <div class=\"section\" style=\"margin-top:2rem;\">\n        <div class=\"section-rule\">\n          <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n          <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n          <h2 class=\"section-title\">Threads Left Hanging</h2>\n          <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n          <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        </div>\n        <ul class=\"thread-list\">\n          <li><strong>The relic is (probably) the stag.</strong> Winifred's bones are inert; the undying stag mirrors her death-and-restoration exactly. The hunt is the mission now — set for the next night.</li>\n          <li><strong>Brother Larkin, wronged.</strong> A genuinely devout Cainite monk they mind-broke and manhandled over bones they didn't even take. He owes them nothing but the silence they asked for.</li>\n          <li><strong>A ghost in the abbey.</strong> Larkin said it twice on the way out — a ghost walks his halls — and left it unexplained.</li>\n          <li><strong>The Order of Absolution, still circling.</strong> Larkin reveres the Sister of Sorrows, has written to unredeemed Tremere, and wants the Order garrisoned at Shrewsbury.</li>\n          <li><strong>\"Stab it.\"</strong> Eustace and Méabh have a real compact now under the game — and a thing she still will not tell him, kept under her dress.</li>\n          <li><strong>Méabh and Oscar, closer than expected.</strong> Two exiles who miss a home. Eustace noticed, and did not love noticing.</li>\n          <li><strong>Dylan, listening.</strong> The Welsh Gangrel keeps drifting to the edge of the coterie's private moments.</li>\n        </ul>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- ══════════ SESSION TWO ══════════ -->\n    <div class=\"session-block\">\n      <div class=\"session-masthead\">\n        <div class=\"sm-left\">\n          <span class=\"session-num\">Session Two</span>\n          <span class=\"session-name\">The Commission, the Court, and the Stag That Would Not Die</span>\n        </div>\n        <span class=\"session-date\">London to Shrewsbury · 1242</span>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"session-standfirst\">The reward for surviving the ship was another errand. On their knees before the prince, the coterie were handed a quiet, deniable commission — find certain relics before the wrong hands do — and sent west to a border lord's court, where a warm welcome, an undying stag, and a grown man aching to be made a monster all waited around the same table. Oscar rode with them and, this night, kept his silence.</p>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">I.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The God in the Room</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">They ended Session One on their knees, and began the next the same way. <strong>Thomas</strong>'s head still lay on the flagstones where it had fallen, jaw slack, lifeless. <strong>Mithras</strong> was in the room — less seen than felt, a pressure at the back of the neck; when <strong>Tomisława</strong> tried to lift her eyes to his face there was a light coming off it like the sun through closed lids, and looking too long cost her real pain. He told <strong>Richard de Worde</strong> that he trusted him, and would see the coterie rewarded handsomely for their service. <em>The matter ahead is of great import — not only to me, but to all Avalon.</em> Then, almost as an afterthought, he healed them: every hurt of the ship and the beach closed at a word. He turned, and the room dimmed to a comfortable, shadowed dark.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">Eustace rolled Composure + Subterfuge to keep his delight at the butchery off his face — five successes, his Ingénue doing quiet work. De Worde noticed nothing.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Mithras</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Richard de Worde</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Thomas</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">II.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">De Worde's Commission</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Left alone with the coterie and the body, <strong>Richard de Worde</strong> — Mithras's spymaster, half his face slumped and melted, one eye lost beneath it — laid out what only he and the prince had known until now. Constantinople has fallen; war rages across the nations; whoever endures stands to shape Cainite society for a long age. Mithras is mighty, but he is one Cainite, and the <strong>Rose Treaty</strong> he granted in mercy has left the rebel Dukes circling. <em>We cannot afford leaks in the ship</em>, he said, and looked at Thomas. The task: certain courts across Avalon are believed to hold <strong>relics</strong> — objects that, in ambitious hands, or worse the <strong>Tremere</strong>'s, could be turned against the prince. He could give them locations and little else — what the relics are, even de Worde does not know. Secure them for Mithras where loyalty allows; where it does not, abscond with them or destroy them — and never let any of it lead back to London.</p>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Their cover is their unimportance: young official envoys, beneath notice. First stop, <strong>Shrewsbury</strong>, in the fief of Gloucester — Gloucester itself held by the loyal <strong>Duchess Seren</strong>, Shrewsbury by her own childe: an honourable old warrior, the <strong>Earl Ostler Blackwell</strong>, also loyal to the prince. A soft opening, de Worde implied, precisely because the names after it are not. When it is done they are to go to the scriptorium at Oxford and ask to see a homily <em>about the Pheasant</em>; someone will find them there, take their report, and give them the next name. Eustace pressed — <em>this seems almost too simple, a test</em> — and got a spymaster's smile: <em>the road tests all of us. We are hoping it is simple. We are hoping it is discreet.</em></p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">\"This will likely be your last task,\" de Worde said. \"A simple envoy — for better or worse.\"</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Richard de Worde</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">III.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">Provisioning</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Downstairs, in a salon of their own, the coterie turned to the practicalities of the road: a mobile haven of four strong wagons dressed up between them — a layer of grave-soil under slatted boards in Tomisława's, a well-fed horse drawing <strong>Méabh</strong>'s — with the coterie's shared means spent on luxury and armour, the retinue standing in for watchmen.</p>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\"><strong>Tomisława</strong> used the London nights to fish. A round of careful conversation turned up the Tremere's political weakness — no status, no numbers, wholly dependent on more powerful patrons who merely tolerate them — and she began drafting letters she would not see answered for months, including one, clan and nature concealed, to a magus of <strong>House Tytalus</strong>, whose long feud with the Tremere she means to turn to use. <strong>Eustace</strong>, for his part, went looking not for Kindred but for the <strong>Fae</strong> — a friend of that country by his descent from Marconius, and by inclination — for a different reading of the road ahead.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">The one stain Tomisława carried from the barn she cleared here with a remorse roll — one success, no degeneration, no lasting mark.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">IV.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Forest of Arden</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Six nights out of London at a good pace: the road as far as Gloucester, and then a day or two where the road simply stops and the last of the way is heavy wood — the <strong>Forest of Arden</strong>, one of the few stretches of England the Romans never cut a road through. <strong>Méabh</strong> took the lead. For two nights unseen dogs howled and paced them through the dark — never glimpsed, their number never holding still, moving in no way a natural pack moves; a bird sent to count them came back none the wiser. The coterie took them for the fiendish black hounds of folk-tale, omens more than animals, and they fell silent only as the land opened onto Shrewsbury: a fortified town on the Welsh march, a battle-outpost with unfriendly neighbours, lately and uneasily quiet. Arriving, <strong>Méabh</strong> would not leave <strong>Scéalán</strong> with the wagons but brought the wolf in at her heel, against every etiquette of a lord's hall — and in a keep where every Cainite is known to the rest and nothing is hidden, the Beast in it marked her, and gave a little of her Gangrel blood away.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Scéalán</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">V.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">Blackwell's Hall</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Inside the keep they were given rooms and a briefing. Every Cainite in the domain knows of the others, so there was nothing to hide: their host <strong>Ostler Blackwell</strong>, who truly rules here; <strong>Lord Gareth Arundel</strong>, whose family's castle this nominally is, ghouled and being tested for the Embrace; Gareth's mother the <strong>Lady Henrietta</strong>, mortal and entirely aware; a Welsh guest named <strong>Dylan</strong>, Cainite and commoner both; and, unseen, a <strong>Brother Larkin</strong>, a monk who keeps an abbey in the town. When the briefing came to Gareth's promised Embrace, Tomisława laid a hand on the <strong>Graf</strong>'s shoulder — she may touch him; he may not touch her — and felt the small, suppressed flinch the subject still costs him; she clocked it, and let it pass. Then she took the moment to school <strong>Méabh</strong> in etiquette — how a Welsh commoner is of no account twice over, and how, being their host's guest, he is nonetheless owed the minimum courtesy — and Méabh, once she grasped that a lesson was underway, nodded with great seriousness.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">Planning in private where the relic might sit — Blackwell's hall, or Larkin's abbey — Tomisława reached for a plan and found none; Eustace's flat \"exactly\" shut the fumble down, and it showed on her face.</em>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">They were announced into the great hall — the <strong>Graf</strong> at Tomisława's side, Méabh behind, Eustace under his own old title — where the court waited around a long table, a fire kept at a careful distance, the walls hung with tapestries of the Arden, of nature, and of some grand mythic battle full of lion-and-dragon things. Well-dressed mortals rose as they entered: some for show, some for the table.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Gareth</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Henrietta</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Dylan</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">the Graf</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">VI.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">Feats and Vanity</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\"><strong>Blackwell</strong> welcomed them on the prince's behalf, apologised for the want of a proper feast — <em>you have caught us at a moment</em> — and offered his triumphs in its place. Kuncze, one of the Graf's men, was set to spar him so he could narrate a siege he had once broken by riding out at night rather than waiting for the walls to fall; the old lord was genuinely good, and pleased the bout lasted exactly as long as the telling. Whether to feed at all was its own tightrope — refuse the offered vessels and slight the host's hospitality, take too freely and slight his means — so they partook with care. Tomisława took a lady-in-waiting's wrist to her lips, briefly, for the form of it, and murmured to Méabh — who was feeding messily and trying to cover it — the night's first commandment of court: <em>whenever it is free to do so, always indulge a great man's vanity.</em></p>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Worked up an appetite, Blackwell asked leave to feed on the man he had just fought, and drank rather more than he meant to before catching himself. Asked where she was from, Tomisława gave the forests of the East, and turned the question to Andrzej, her tracker, who told a plainly terrifying story — monstrous things in the trees, meticulously avoided — in the flat voice of a man describing a rake left in the grass. Blackwell, who knows a real hunter when he hears one, leaned in. Méabh, bored by the war-stories, came sharply awake at the discovery that this lord was more predator than general.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Kuncze</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Andrzej</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">VII.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Stag That Would Not Die</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\"><strong>Dylan</strong> prodded him toward the story he had left unfinished. <strong>Blackwell</strong> pointed down a wall of hunting-trophies to one very large, very empty mount. He had taken a stag once, the greatest head anyone had seen — and the head had vanished from its place. Stolen, he assumed; he questioned his people hard. Weeks on, deep in the woods, he found it again — the same head, as if it had climbed down and walked out to spite him. He killed the stag a second time, took the head himself, and woke the next night to find it gone and the beast alive in the trees. Each time it seemed angrier. <em>Have you ever met a stag that fought back? This one did.</em> It had cost him some of his best dogs, and a houndmaster, and still hangs on no wall. If they doubted him, they were welcome to come and see — and so entertainment and feast were folded into one: a hunt, together, in a night or two. The Graf was delighted.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">A stag that will not stay dead, in a wood the road refuses to enter. The first thread of the relic they were sent to find — or simply the Forest of Arden being what it is.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Dylan</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">the Graf</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">VIII.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Ghoul and the Kiasyd</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\"><strong>Gareth</strong> could not stop staring at Eustace, and finally asked outright whether he was Cainite — a grown man who has never seen one, kept ghouled and made to wait years for a gift he plainly aches for. Learning that <strong>Eustace</strong> had been Embraced as a child — a pre-adolescent, frozen years short of manhood — <em>I was just too special</em>, he offered — Gareth turned the knife the other way: he would not want to be stuck like that, he said, and what manner of sire makes a child of a man? He named Eustace's maker <em>a real bastard of a sire</em>, and Eustace, stung for once, could only answer that to point such a thing out was rude. Around them the room turned uneasy — the shadows moving wrong at Eustace's back, the cold fae strangeness of his blood pressing at the edges of the candlelight.</p>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Off to one side, <strong>Dylan</strong> had crouched into a wordless conversation with <strong>Scéalán</strong>, trading small animal sounds; <strong>Méabh</strong>, raising her blood to listen in, warmed to the one soul in the hall who spoke her language — and let him tell her, cheekily, that the wolf preferred his company.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Gareth</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Dylan</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Scéalán</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">IX.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">Sires, and the Graf</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Gareth, who takes the <strong>Graf</strong> for a mortal on the same road he is — kept close and readied for the Embrace — drew <strong>Tomisława</strong> onto the making of vampires: what it is to be sired, and what she is to her own sire. She gave him more than she gives most: <em>I would not subject the Graf to the cruelty I suffered.</em> A line about her own making, and a promise about her husband's, left to stand without further explanation.</p>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">It was out of that conversation, and not the quarrel with Eustace, that her clan came up. Asked, she offered the truth she rarely does: <em>I may be the only one of my kind for many leagues — but I am Tzimisce.</em> <strong>Blackwell</strong>, mid-draught on a hunter, stopped and looked up; <strong>Dylan</strong>'s brow rose; the name landed. The old lord found the careful way to circle the ugly question, and she spared him having to ask it: <em>I do not partake in the crafting of flesh, if that is what you are wondering.</em> He apologised for the near-offence all the same.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Gareth</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Dylan</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">the Graf</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">X.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Saint and the Order</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Between all this, the useful thread. Asking after <strong>Brother Larkin</strong>, Tomisława drew out the shape of the place. <strong>Lady Henrietta</strong>, who had watched the whole evening in near-silence, cleared her throat and told the story properly: <strong>Saint Winifred</strong>, a Welsh maiden beheaded by the suitor <strong>Caradog</strong> for refusing him and choosing the veil; a spring risen where her head fell; her head rejoined and her life restored by her great-uncle <strong>Saint Beuno</strong>, who called the earth up to swallow the murderer. Winifred lived to old age, and her relics turned the town's once-unconsecrated abbey into a thriving one — a pilgrim site, if a hard one to reach, since the Romans never built a road that way.</p>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Which is where it tightened. The road has lately been made safe by the <strong>Order of Absolution</strong> — the same knightly order the coterie met burning on a beach a week ago — who guard pilgrims and, pointedly, protect relics. Brother Larkin is a zealot for them and has been pressing Blackwell to garrison them at Shrewsbury; the lord has not agreed, and they have not been seen here in months. A domain with a rumoured relic, an abbey full of holy bone, and an order that hunts the red-masked man they lied about: Tomisława asked to see the abbey, and Henrietta agreed to lead them there herself, after the hunt. She will need a heavy veil to go.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Henrietta</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Gareth</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">XI.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">Teeth in the Room</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\"><strong>Eustace</strong> was not finished with Gareth's slight, nor with the host who had let it pass. He drew himself up on his family's name — genuine, and prominent — and called the thing what it was: a lord disrespecting a guest under his own roof. It became, in the end, a duel — charm and standing against the old lord's pride — and Eustace critted it, six successes, and was maddeningly, unanswerably <em>right</em>. <strong>Blackwell</strong> wanted to turn it into blood; everyone in the room could see the predator behind his eyes weigh it. But a sword drawn on a snide boy only makes the boy's point, and Blackwell cares, above almost anything, for his dignitas. He swallowed it. The shadows stretched long across the floor as he bent, and the lord bowed, and apologised to his own court for a lapse of etiquette — meaning the shape of it, if not the substance.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">Méabh watched it the way a rabbit watches a hound: certain Eustace had just shown a room full of predators that he has teeth, and that they would not forget it.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ostler Blackwell</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Gareth</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">XII.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Stoat</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Before the night broke up, <strong>Méabh</strong> drew <strong>Eustace</strong> aside. Sometimes a dog does need to be shown what it did, she allowed — but he had also just shown a hall of hunters that he can bite, and they will count him a threat now. Was he fast enough, she asked; strong enough? <em>Then be clever enough to find refuge.</em> She turned it over the way she turns everything over, in rabbits: the clever, hunted things that endure by being unremarkable and quick to ground — indispensable and dispensable at once, all of them alike, alive precisely because no one can tell one from the next. In every dealing that night, she told him, he had played the rabbit.</p>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\"><strong>Eustace</strong> would not have it. He is no rabbit, he said — and neither is she; and when she asked whether he thought rabbits so poor a thing, he answered that he would rather be something singular. He named himself a <em>stoat</em>: small, yes, but a killer, and wearing a pelt worth far more than anyone expects. Méabh looked genuinely pleased. She would not climb into a trap in his place — her own survival comes first — but if he ever went looking for wolves, she and <strong>Scéalán</strong> would be there. <em>Very well, then — little stoat</em>, she said, and rose to her full height, and told Scéalán on the way out to be mindful of him.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Scéalán</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- THREADS -->\n      <div class=\"section\" style=\"margin-top:2rem;\">\n        <div class=\"section-rule\">\n          <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n          <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n          <h2 class=\"section-title\">Threads Left Hanging</h2>\n          <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n          <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        </div>\n        <ul class=\"thread-list\">\n          <li><strong>The relic at Shrewsbury.</strong> Somewhere in this domain is what they were sent for — on Blackwell's walls, in Larkin's abbey, or out in the wood with the stag. They do not yet know which.</li>\n          <li><strong>The stag that will not die.</strong> A great head that keeps leaving its mount and returning to the forest, angrier each time, in a wood the road avoids. The hunt is set for a night or two hence.</li>\n          <li><strong>The Order of Absolution, again.</strong> The order from the beach guards this pilgrim road and protects relics; Brother Larkin wants them garrisoned here. The coterie still carries the lie about Ludan.</li>\n          <li><strong>Saint Winifred's abbey.</strong> Holy relics, consecrated ground, and Henrietta's promise to lead them in — veiled — after the hunt.</li>\n          <li><strong>Eustace has teeth now.</strong> He forced a lord to bow before his own court. Blackwell will remember it, and so will Gareth.</li>\n          <li><strong>Tomisława's letters.</strong> Quiet lines cast toward the Tremere's weaknesses and a Tytalus magus, months from any reply.</li>\n          <li><strong>Méabh and Dylan.</strong> A Welsh Gangrel who speaks to her wolf and keeps his own counsel about his sire.</li>\n          <li><strong>The man who wants it.</strong> Gareth Arundel, ghouled and aching for an Embrace he has been made to wait for — and, after the envoy forced his lord to bow, nursing a grudge.</li>\n        </ul>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- ══════════ SESSION ONE ══════════ -->\n    <div class=\"session-block\">\n      <div class=\"session-masthead\">\n        <div class=\"sm-left\">\n          <span class=\"session-num\">Session One</span>\n          <span class=\"session-name\">The Ship, the Order, and the Head of Thomas</span>\n        </div>\n        <span class=\"session-date\">Homeward from Norway · 1242</span>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"session-standfirst\">The errand was simple and beneath them: carry a chest they knew better than to open to a band of Cainites in Norway, travel in secret, make a good impression. By every account it went well — better than well. Then, a week and a bit at sea, the ship stopped moving.</p>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">I.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Hold</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\"><strong>Eustace</strong> woke first, to thunder above and screaming men, rain pooling pink through the deck seams and a hatch banging open onto lethal daylight. Bronisława already had her mistress's lid off and gave him a look of pure resignation. A raider came down the steps; Eustace caught his gaze and mesmerized him back up the stairs, rationalized so cleanly the man thought it his own idea. <strong>Méabh</strong> and <strong>Tomisława</strong> woke next — Méabh whispering Gaelic at <strong>Scéalán</strong> to defend her, Tomisława asking for a water barrel.</p>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">She opened her wrist, plunged her hand in, and burned three aggravated for a third of a mile of koldunic sight — saw their ship beached, a second hull alongside, their own sail burning — then critted Elemental Grasp and pulled the raider ship off her hull on a swell, stranding the rest. She stayed in the barrel for the fight, narrating. The rest was <strong>Oscar</strong>'s: woken early and hard out of torpor, Blood Surge and Claymore, he took the last raider's torch-arm off at the shoulder and drained him with Brutal Feed, ending fed to zero and cheerful. Above, the raiders scattered; armored cavalry came down the hill.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Scéalán</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Bronisława</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">II.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">Interlude — The Voyage Down</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">A flashback to the nights before the crash: nothing to do in a box but talk, one topic each. <strong>Oscar</strong> asked about mortal family and got least of anyone — Tomisława confirmed only a nobleman husband; Méabh slid into myth; Oscar went quiet: <em>I was a knight. I took off.</em> <strong>Tomisława</strong> asked whether Méabh's tradition worked <em>fire</em> and got a blank look, and was deeply disappointed. <strong>Eustace</strong> asked about breeding and sized everyone up — filed Méabh a fraud (<em>was your wolf a noble too?</em>) and Tomisława, after both critted, as genuinely noble and permanently <em>queen of the swamp</em>. On God, Oscar warned Méabh to be careful who she tells: people must believe the narrative for it to have power.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">The armistice between the peers is settled here: her rank respected, her country a bog. Méabh's kinship survives on Tomisława's credit, not her own.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">III.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">Nightfall, and Ludan</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">The sun went down; the mast kept burning. A lid came off a box nobody had accounted for and <strong>Ludan</strong> sat up in it — wooden mask, carved mustache, red hair, a box of dead rodents, aboard the whole voyage unseen. <strong>Eustace</strong> couldn't get eye contact through the mask and worked out Nosferatu on politics. Facing the fire, <strong>Eustace</strong> and <strong>Oscar</strong> both frenzied and went over the side; Ludan yelled <em>fucking Catholics</em> and followed; <strong>Méabh</strong> ran. <strong>Tomisława did not frenzy</strong> — she stood on the burning deck and studied the flames, fascinated, with Bronisława beside her. She and Méabh read the riders' heraldry: the Order of Absolution.</p>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Down the coast, Oscar hauled Eustace bodily through the surf by the scruff — <em>didn't they teach you to swim in Hell, demon child</em> — the shadows thickening as Eustace seethed. On the beach Ludan gave Oscar a fox-carved coin (<em>in Cambridge, ask for a bolt of red</em>) and Eustace a knife, and vanished into the woods.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Ludan</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Bronisława</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">IV.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Order</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Tomisława elected to present rather than hide — veiled, claiming an illness of the sight, led by the hand by Bronisława. <strong>Guarin of Jersey</strong> lifted his visor, apologised for the butchery, and produced a boarding ramp, offering to host her \"cousin and herself.\" Up the hill rode mounted knights and one nun, <strong>Mary, Sister of Sorrow</strong>. Tomisława's attempt to conceal Ludan went to a bestial failure and her hunger rose. Oscar read Guarin as sincere and his ornate armour as under-worn; Méabh read <strong>sigils</strong> in it belonging to no school she knows. They rode to a barn in Suffolk.</p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Guarin</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Mary</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Bronisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Scéalán</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">V.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Barn</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Hungry, holding a lie together, <strong>Tomisława</strong> took Bronisława by the wrist and asked <strong>Méabh</strong> to stop her once she'd had a sip. She could not stop; Méabh was not strong enough. <strong>Oscar</strong> broke the latch of her jaw the way you would with a dog, pried her off, and held her through a full hunger frenzy while she thrashed and wept and begged — in front of <strong>Eustace</strong>. She named the shame herself. Méabh fetched <strong>Mary</strong>, who walked in knowing exactly what she faced, laid a hand on Tomisława's forehead, gave three willpower and an end to it — and bled from beneath her habit doing it. Then <strong>Guarin</strong> undid a gauntlet and offered his wrist: <em>I have blood to spare.</em> She drank, down to hunger 1.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">By the Bond Slave flaw, one drink is the whole bond. She stood up in that barn blood bound to a knight of the Order of Absolution, roughly an hour after meeting him — and then compelled Bronisława to sleep so the rest could speak.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Mary</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Guarin</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Bronisława</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">VI.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">What The Order Wanted</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\"><strong>Guarin</strong> asked their business; Tomisława gave him the husband in London — which cost her nothing, as she'd said it before the bond. It was <strong>Oscar</strong> who added, unprompted, that they work for Mithras. Then Guarin admitted the Order had not come for bandits: they were hunting a red-haired man, possibly masked. He handed the questioning to <strong>Mary</strong> — <em>we believe he has something very important</em> — and the coterie said nothing.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">The hinge of the session, unclocked at the table: Tomisława was bound to Guarin, who could have compelled the truth — but the one dangerous question was asked by Mary, to whom she is not bound. The secret survived on which of the two happened to be talking.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Guarin</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Mary</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">VII.</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Debrief</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Called in by <strong>Thomas</strong> in London — with <strong>Richard de Worde</strong> present, which was new. The spymaster's questions were gentle and were not gentle. They gave him Ludan; he accepted it as coincidence. Tomisława said her husband knew, and had been at court the whole time; Richard said <em>we've had eyes on him.</em> Then a door opened and the room filled with light like sunlight that did not burn. Everyone knelt. Metal met meat, and the head of <strong>Thomas</strong> arrived in their peripheral vision. <em>Rise</em>, said <strong>Mithras</strong>. Richard claimed the fault — the rat was Thomas, the envoy revealed nothing. <em>Our enemies have revealed themselves. And these ones can be trusted?</em> — <em>Proceed.</em> Then, mildly: <em>Now this means we can start the real work.</em></p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">In scene</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Thomas</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Richard de Worde</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Mithras</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- THREADS -->\n      <div class=\"section\" style=\"margin-top:2rem;\">\n        <div class=\"section-rule\">\n          <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n          <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n          <h2 class=\"section-title\">Threads Left Hanging</h2>\n          <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n          <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        </div>\n        <ul class=\"thread-list\">\n          <li><strong>Ludan and the bolt of red.</strong> They know where to find him — Cambridge — and lied to a knightly order about it.</li>\n          <li><strong>What Ludan has.</strong> Mary believes it matters, and Mary is Salubri, which narrows the field. She would probably tell them if asked.</li>\n          <li><strong>The bestial failure.</strong> Tomisława's concealment of Ludan failed badly; Guarin and Mary may have registered that she answered a slightly different question.</li>\n          <li><strong>\"The real work.\"</strong> Whatever the chest bought in Norway, Richard considers the envoy phase concluded.</li>\n          <li><strong>The leak.</strong> Thomas is dead, but <em>certain people</em> is plural.</li>\n          <li><strong>Guarin's armour.</strong> Someone with real, unfamiliar power made or gave it — sigils of no known school.</li>\n          <li><strong>The blood bond.</strong> Tomisława is bound to Guarin of Jersey. She cannot refuse him a direct question; he does not appear to know he holds the leash, nor what she is.</li>\n          <li><strong>Stains from the ship</strong> — Oscar's, for the lie to the Order; Tomisława's, for behaving shamefully before her peers, which she cleared with a remorse roll at the top of Session Two.</li>\n        </ul>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- ══════════ SESSION ZERO ══════════ -->\n    <div class=\"session-block\">\n      <div class=\"session-masthead\">\n        <div class=\"sm-left\">\n          <span class=\"session-num\">Session Zero</span>\n          <span class=\"session-name\">The Envoy Assembled</span>\n        </div>\n        <span class=\"session-date\">London · 1242 · under Mithras</span>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"session-standfirst\">Where the coterie, its premise, and its errand were established — the arrangement going in, before Session One cost any of them anything. Revised in light of what the first night revealed.</p>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">§</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Premise</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">Four young Cainites bound together in the service of <strong>Mithras</strong>, Prince of London, and sent out as his envoy — the errand-runners trusted to fetch what no hired mortal can be trusted with. Dangerous, low-status work most Kindred do not survive to profit from, and for that reason one of the few ladders a hungry neonate can climb. <strong>Tomisława</strong> did not choose the other three; she chose Mithras, and was overwhelmed by him — the first time she understood beings of his stature existed. When he said <em>jump</em>, she asked <em>how high.</em></p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">Established</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag npc\">Mithras</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">§</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Coterie</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">The arrangement going in: two high-clan aristocrats at the top, two displaced exiles beneath them, and Méabh moving across the seam. <strong>Tomisława z Białowieży</strong>, Tzimisce koldun of the Road of Kings — the occult and infrastructural core. <strong>Eustace the Kiasyd</strong>, fae-touched Lasombra of Marconius's line, Road of Kings — her one true peer, by armistice. <strong>Méabh</strong>, Lhiannan hiding as high-clan kin, Road of the Beast — guide, liar, and the coterie's hinge, whom Tomisława holds <em>out-of-category</em>. <strong>Oscar \"Disgraced\" Castro Avis</strong>, fallen Brujah knight of Lisbon, Road of Humanity — the muscle, and the group's reluctant conscience, whom Tomisława files as her inferior for having thrown his rank away.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">Full portraits and profiles are on the <a href=\"#coterie\" style=\"color:var(--blood-deep);\">Coterie</a> page.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">Established</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">Scéalán</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">§</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">The Errand to Norway</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">The framing mission: carry a small chest — one they knew better than to open — to a band of Cainites in Norway, learn what could be learned, make a good impression, and above all travel in secret. What was there: Cainites with the trappings of Vikings, mostly <strong>Gangrel</strong>, holding hard against a Christianity that has been closing around them. They work the old ways — magic through the land, worked <em>with</em> it. Their mortals know what they are and are fed from directly, throat to mouth, often dosed with something that makes the blood strange, a shaman presiding; and women hold more power among them than in the courts of Europe.</p>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">For Tomisława, a place where earth-drawn blood sorcery is unremarkable and a woman with authority is no curiosity — she had a better time there than she is likely to have in London. The others fared worse on a telling gradient: Oscar found them charming and hollow, Eustace found their reverence for cleverness more interesting than expected, and Méabh — closest to them in kind — closed up completely.</p>\n        <em class=\"beat\">The delivery went well; the impression was good. The chest is why Thomas is dead. Norway is not a punchline — it is the opening move of whatever \"the real work\" turns out to be.</em>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">Established</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag extra\">The Norse (Gangrel)</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"scene\">\n        <div class=\"scene-head\"><span class=\"scene-idx\">§</span><h4 class=\"scene-title\">On the Table for Session Two</h4></div>\n        <p class=\"scene-body\">The coterie is off the rails and setting its own pace; travel is on them now, and the road — not the courtroom — is the dangerous part. Players' wishes: Oscar's wants the tragic, depressive side rather than the bastard; Eustace's wants to complicate him beyond the petulant ambassador-child and untangle something with Méabh; Méabh's is a long game, with any hint of her sire the priority; Tomisława's is fire and the Veins of the Earth. The GM is building a non-Christian throughline for every character. <strong>Two weeks. The 31st.</strong></p>\n        <div class=\"scene-tags\">\n          <span class=\"tags-label\">Looking ahead</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Oscar</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Eustace</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Méabh</span>\n          <span class=\"char-tag coterie\">Tomisława</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    </div>\n</div><!-- .document -->\n", "coterie": "<div class=\"document\">\n  <div class=\"doc-inner\">\n\n\n    <div class=\"page-eyebrow\">The London Envoy · In the service of Mithras</div>\n    <h1 class=\"page-title\">The Coterie</h1>\n    <p class=\"page-subtitle\">Four young Cainites, homeward from Norway · 1242</p>\n    <p class=\"page-lede\">The errand-runners Mithras trusts to fetch what no hired mortal can be trusted with — dangerous, low-status work that most Kindred do not survive long enough to profit from, and precisely for that reason one of the few ladders a hungry neonate can climb. They have been a coterie perhaps two years, a handful of jobs deep: long enough to know the basics of one another, not long enough to be friends by default. Session One was the first night it cost any of them anything.</p>\n\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">The Four</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- TOMISŁAWA -->\n      <div class=\"char-card\">\n        <div class=\"char-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/tomi-full.webp\" alt=\"Tomisława z Białowieży\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Tomisława z Białowieży</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"char-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"pc-name\">Tomisława <span class=\"handle\">z Białowieży · \"Queen of the Swamp\"</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"pc-subtitle\">The occult and infrastructural core</p>\n          <div class=\"stat-row\">\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Clan <strong>Tzimisce</strong></span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Koldun</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip road\">Road <strong>of Kings</strong></span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">10th Gen · BP 2</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Earth · Water · Air</span>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">A brilliant, fragile political glass cannon walking the rigid Road of Kings with an aura of supreme, chilling self-mastery. She is the coterie's engine: she opened her wrist into a barrel of drinking water, put her senses five hundred metres out through the sea, found the raider hull grinding against her own, and pulled it off on a swell — the single decisive act of the fight, performed while everyone else was stabbing people. She is also the most fragile body at the table, and Session One demonstrated that at greater length than she would like.</p>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">She did not choose these three. She chose Mithras, and they came attached. Her element is water; her appetite is fire — and she now knows her out-of-category peer cannot help her reach it.</p>\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <div class=\"callout-label\">Full Sheet</div>\n            <p class=\"callout-body\">Her complete dossier — attributes, disciplines, rituals, household and herd — lives in the <a href=\"#household\" style=\"color:var(--blood-deep);\">Household of Białowieży</a> document.</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- EUSTACE -->\n      <div class=\"char-card\">\n        <div class=\"char-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/eustace-full.webp\" alt=\"Eustace the Kiasyd\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Eustace · the Kiasyd</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"char-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"pc-name\">Eustace <span class=\"handle\">the Kiasyd · \"I'm not like the other children\"</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"pc-subtitle\">Her one true peer</p>\n          <div class=\"stat-row\">\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Bloodline <strong>Kiasyd</strong></span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Line of Marconius</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip road\">Road <strong>of Kings</strong></span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Dominate · Oblivion</span>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">A fae-touched Lasombra embraced into a twelve-year-old's body more than four decades ago — a creature past fifty wearing a child's frozen shape. Proud of the clan, unbothered by the faith (<em>it's all real, and none of it's real is how I view it</em>), and by turns petulant and coldly calculated: a sadist whose cruelty points toward mastery rather than appetite. He spent one fight patiently sawing into a man's skull with a knife and Strength 1 to get at the brain. London's Lasombra keep him at arm's length and post him as a local watcher; he is content with the leash, because distance is room to manoeuvre.</p>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\"><strong>To Tomisława:</strong> her equal by armistice — two absolute convictions of superiority that have agreed not to fight. He has conceded she is genuinely, if provincially, noble; he has also, now, <em>watched her lose</em> — wept and thrashing in Oscar's arms — and said nothing about it. The next time he asks her for Vicissitude, he will be asking from a position he did not hold before.</p>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- MÉABH -->\n      <div class=\"char-card\">\n        <div class=\"char-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/meabh-full.webp\" alt=\"Méabh\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Méabh · of the Irish groves</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"char-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"pc-name\">Méabh <span class=\"handle\">\"Tomisława's Irish cousin\"</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"pc-subtitle\">Guide, liar, and the coterie's hinge</p>\n          <div class=\"stat-row\">\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Bloodline <strong>Lhiannan</strong></span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip road\">Road <strong>of the Beast</strong></span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Rune-magic</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Animal pack</span>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">One of the last of a doomed bloodline — carrying a fragment of her sire's soul and a literal piece of her forest, hunted by the Gangrel who would end her line, split from her sire in a raid and hoping to find him again. She walks the Road of the Beast, hunts with a pack, and works her magic as runes cut into her own skin, matriarchal rites she traces to the Crone. On the surface she is sweet, innocent, mild-mannered; underneath she is five steps ahead and wants to be the predator in every room. A spider playing at being a lamb.</p>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\"><strong>To Tomisława:</strong> the kinswoman she invented. By the plain rules Méabh should rank low; instead Tomisława lifts her clean off the ladder and holds her <em>out-of-category</em> — a peer on the old powers of the earth, unmeasured on all else. Their cover is welded together: Méabh's claim to kinship survives on Tomisława's credit, which makes each a hostage of the other. Her heart, though, belongs to Oscar, not her patron.</p>\n          <div class=\"companion\">\n            <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n              <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/scealan-full.webp\" alt=\"Scéalán the red wolf\">\n            </div>\n            <div>\n              <p class=\"companion-name\">Scéalán — the red wolf</p>\n              <p class=\"companion-body\">Her familiar: a red wolf grown large on her blood, named for one of the hounds of the Fianna cycle. Blood-fed and battle-scarred; he intercepted two raiders in the hold and dragged one down by the calf so Oscar could finish it. Where Méabh goes, he is put between her and the room.</p>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- OSCAR -->\n      <div class=\"char-card\">\n        <div class=\"char-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/oscar-full.webp\" alt=\"Oscar Castro Avis\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Oscar \"Disgraced\" Castro Avis</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"char-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"pc-name\">Oscar Castro Avis <span class=\"handle\">\"Disgraced\" · the fallen knight of Lisbon</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"pc-subtitle\">The muscle, and the group's reluctant conscience</p>\n          <div class=\"stat-row\">\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Clan <strong>Brujah</strong></span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">High Clan</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip road\">Road <strong>of Humanity 6</strong></span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Potence · Blood Surge</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Claymore</span>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">A Brujah of Lisbon — philosopher-kings, not rebels, in these nights. A fourth or fifth son raised to the sword and to real purpose, a knight who believed in it, undone by scandal and a lord's betrayal and dragged out of that ruin by a sire who eventually tired of his self-pity and sent him to the Isles with a hard instruction: <em>go find yourself.</em> His nature is honourable; his demeanour is nihilistic. He reads people well, and is a violent man trying to stop reaching for violence as the easy answer. His surcoat is a black Templar standard — black paint laid over what, up close, you can tell used to be a cross.</p>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">Session One was largely his fight: Blood Surge, Potence and Claymore through a hold full of raiders, ending fed to zero on drugged blood and cheerful. Then he broke Tomisława's jaw open to pry her off Bronisława and held her through the frenzy — competent, unsentimental, unpaid.</p>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\"><strong>To Tomisława:</strong> the one she files as her <em>inferior</em> — not for clan (he shares her rank exactly) but for <em>abdication</em>, a nobleman playing hired sword. She refuses to see that they are mirror childer, both cast out by hard sires to remake themselves at a distance: she is what that process looks like when it succeeds, he is what it looks like mid-collapse. He is quietly becoming the group's exasperated uncle — and something with no drawer in her filing system.</p>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- SHAPE OF THE GROUP -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">The Shape of the Group</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"char-bio\">The coterie's own reckoning: two high-clan aristocrats at the top, two displaced exiles beneath them, and Méabh moving across the seam. What Session One demonstrated is that the reckoning and the actual load-bearing structure are not the same object. Under pressure the traffic did not run along the status lines — it ran <strong>Eustace and Oscar</strong> in the water, hauling and mocking each other; <strong>Oscar and Tomisława</strong> on the barn floor; <strong>Méabh and a nun</strong>.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout blood\">\n        <div class=\"callout-label\">A Seam Between the Peers</div>\n        <p class=\"callout-body\">Tomisława and Eustace agree they are the top pair — and disagree on how to rank the bottom one. Eustace lumps Méabh and Oscar together as beneath them both; Tomisława files Oscar low but holds Méabh out-of-category. Méabh's cover now rests entirely on Tomisława's credibility, which gives Tomisława a hostage and Méabh a bomb. And Oscar — the one they agree to file as \"help\" — spent the session being the most consequential body in the group, and is not staying filed.</p>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    </div>\n</div><!-- .document -->\n", "home": "<div class=\"document\">\n  <div class=\"doc-inner\">\n\n    <header class=\"cover-head\">\n      <div class=\"cover-eyebrow\">A Vampire: The Dark Ages Chronicle</div>\n      <h1 class=\"cover-title\">War of Princes</h1>\n      <p class=\"cover-sub\">London · 1242 · under the domain of Mithras</p>\n      <p class=\"cover-kicker\">The archive of the London Envoy</p>\n    </header>\n\n    <div class=\"flourish\"></div>\n\n    <p class=\"lede\">Four young Cainites in the service of Mithras, Prince of London — the errand-runners trusted to fetch what no hired mortal can be trusted with. This is the record of that service: who they are, the people the road has given them and lied to, and everything the night has cost them. Homeward from Norway, and a burning ship, and the head of Thomas.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"flourish\"></div>\n\n    <div class=\"grid\">\n      <a class=\"card\" href=\"#setting\">\n        <span class=\"num\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"cat\">London · 1242 · Mithras</span>\n        <h3>The Setting</h3>\n        <p>The premise and the world of 1242 — the Baronies of Avalon, the Rose Treaty, and the powers that carve the night.</p>\n      </a>\n      <a class=\"card\" href=\"#coterie\">\n        <span class=\"num\">4</span>\n        <span class=\"cat\">The London Envoy</span>\n        <h3>The Coterie</h3>\n        <p>The four young Cainites in Mithras's service — who they are, and where each stands with the others.</p>\n      </a>\n      <a class=\"card\" href=\"#people\">\n        <span class=\"num\">12</span>\n        <span class=\"cat\">Road · Beach · Court · March</span>\n        <h3>Dramatis Personae</h3>\n        <p>The people the envoy has met — the stowaway, the Order of Absolution, the courts of London and Shrewsbury, and the abbey.</p>\n      </a>\n      <a class=\"card\" href=\"#chronicle\">\n        <span class=\"num\">4</span>\n        <span class=\"cat\">A record of the nights</span>\n        <h3>The Chronicle</h3>\n        <p>The session log — most recent first, with the characters present tagged in every scene.</p>\n      </a>\n      <a class=\"card\" href=\"#household\">\n        <span class=\"num\">14</span>\n        <span class=\"cat\">Household of Białowieży</span>\n        <h3>The Household</h3>\n        <p>Tomisława in full — her sheet, her ghoul-husband the Graf, the four allies, and the herd that feeds her.</p>\n      </a>\n    </div>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n", "household": "<div class=\"tab-nav-wrap\">\n  <nav class=\"tab-nav\" role=\"tablist\">\n    <button class=\"tab-btn active\" role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\"  onclick=\"switchTab('tomi')\">Tomisława</button>\n    <button class=\"tab-btn\"        role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"false\" onclick=\"switchTab('graf')\">Graf Światosław</button>\n    <button class=\"tab-btn\"        role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"false\" onclick=\"switchTab('allies')\">The Four Allies</button>\n    <button class=\"tab-btn\"        role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"false\" onclick=\"switchTab('herd')\">The Herd</button>\n  </nav>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"document\">\n\n  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════\n       TAB 1 — TOMISŁAWA\n  ════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->\n  <div id=\"tab-tomi\" class=\"tab-panel active\">\n  <div class=\"doc-inner\">\n\n\n    <header class=\"masthead\">\n      <div>\n        <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-swap\">\n            <img class=\"primary\"   src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/tomi-full.webp\"        alt=\"Portrait of Tomisława z Białowieży\">\n            <img class=\"secondary\" src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/tomi-horror-full.webp\" alt=\"Tomisława in frenzy\">\n          </div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Tomisława z Białowieży · b. 1212, Embraced 1230</div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"name-block\">\n        <div class=\"name-eyebrow\">Tzimisce · 10th Generation · Blood Potency 2</div>\n        <h1 class=\"char-name\">Tomisława<br><span class=\"nickname\">z Białowieży</span></h1>\n        <p class=\"char-subtitle\">Road of Kings · Via Regalis · Chronicle: War of Princes</p>\n        <dl class=\"identity-rows\">\n          <dt>Sire</dt>        <dd>Pękosław z Białowieży</dd>\n          <dt>Embraced</dt>    <dd>1230 · Apparent Age 18 · True Age 30</dd>\n          <dt>Road</dt>        <dd><span class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"tomislawa\" data-part=\"road\"></span></dd>\n          <dt>Moral Guideline</dt><dd><span class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"tomislawa\" data-part=\"guideline\"></span></dd>\n          <dt>Languages</dt>   <dd>Old Polish, English</dd>\n          <dt>Koldunic Bonds</dt><dd>Earth · Water · Air</dd>\n        </dl>\n      </div>\n    </header>\n\n    <!-- BIO -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">On the Subject</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"bio\">\n        <p>Pękosław z Białowieży broke Tomisława down and rebuilt her as an instrument of aristocratic poise and koldunic mastery. Before her transformation was complete, he arranged her marriage to Graf Światosław Odrowąż — a calculated play to secure her mortal mask. She did not leave the Carpathians willingly. The Bondslave flaw chains her to his will, and he commanded it. He sent her out because her success feeds his prestige, her absence reduces strain on local food supply, she may uncover new magical knowledge along ley lines, and she is a potential rival removed. He is at peace with her hatred of him.</p>\n        <p>She travels this perilous 1242 landscape as a brilliant but fragile political glass cannon, walking the rigid Road of Kings with an aura of supreme, chilling self-mastery. She possesses formidable mastery over Dominate, Protean flesh-crafting, and the primal elemental paths of Koldunic Sorcery, yet she is balanced on a razor's edge by profound physical vulnerabilities — including a fatal weakness to a wooden stake and a double-dose of her clan's ancient curses.</p>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- ATTRIBUTES -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">Attributes</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"tomislawa\" data-part=\"attributes\"></div>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- SKILLS -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">Skills</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"tomislawa\" data-part=\"skills\"></div>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- DISCIPLINES -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">Disciplines &amp; Powers</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"tomislawa\" data-part=\"powers\"></div>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- MERITS / FLAWS -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">Merits &amp; Flaws</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"tomislawa\" data-part=\"advantages\"></div>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- FAITH & FIRE -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">Faith &amp; Fire</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"bio\">\n        <p>Raised Catholic but always <em>dvoeverie</em>. She did not abandon Christianity through trauma but through absorption — during her early years among older Tzimisce, she encountered a philosophical framework shaped by the Mongol conquests. The elders observed the Mongols with begrudging respect: vast power, held and then lost. The lesson was not that power is punished, but that it is inherently unstable, and the only meaningful question is how much one can accumulate and how long one can hold it.</p>\n        <p>What she built from that is not a cult. It is a personal religious practice — a shamanic initiation into <em>kolduny</em>, passed down from initiated to initiate, rarely involving more than two people and usually only one. It is not worship. The goal is mastery over earth, air, water, and ultimately fire. The aspiration is to emulate Tiamat in dominion over the elements. She does not bend the knee.</p>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"callout\" style=\"margin-top:1rem;\">\n        <div class=\"callout-label\">Fire</div>\n        <p class=\"callout-body\">Her sire has not mastered Fire. Neither has she. She is terrified of it and drawn to it. Mastering Fire is one of the few ways she could demonstrate a greatness that exceeds Pękosław — but she knows he would claim it as evidence of his excellence in cultivating her. Whether her terror is genuine or a shelter from the risk of trying is unresolved.</p>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- CORE TENSIONS -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">Core Tensions</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"tension\">\n        <div class=\"tension-title\">The Sire's Shadow</div>\n        <p class=\"tension-body\">Pękosław is structurally present in almost everything she does — she carries his tradition, his bond, his design. Any achievement she accomplishes will be read by him as his success. Fire is the only territory that might escape that logic.</p>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"tension\">\n        <div class=\"tension-title\">Inherited Belief versus Her Own</div>\n        <p class=\"tension-body\">She does not always know what she has concluded versus what she has been given. The philosophy of power came from elder discourse. The religious practice came from Pękosław's tradition. Fleshcrafting's absence came from Old Clan aesthetics. What she arrived at alone: the no-touching rule, the specific texture of the hunt, her relationship to Fire.</p>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"tension\">\n        <div class=\"tension-title\">Control and Its Costs</div>\n        <p class=\"tension-body\">Everything she has built is oriented toward mastery. The cost is that mastery forecloses the things that require vulnerability.</p>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- TAROT -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">Tarot Profile</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"tarot-grid\">\n        <div class=\"tarot-card\">\n          <div class=\"tarot-label\">Core Trait</div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-card-name\">Justice (Reversed)</div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-desc\">Lack of accountability; avoiding consequences.</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"tarot-card\">\n          <div class=\"tarot-label\">Goal</div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-card-name\">King of Swords</div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-desc\">Authority, intellectual power, mastery. Decisions made from overwhelming truth and principle.</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"tarot-card\">\n          <div class=\"tarot-label\">Barrier</div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-card-name\">High Priestess (Reversed)</div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-desc\">Information withheld; disconnected from her own intuition and inner voice.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════\n       TAB 2 — GRAF ŚWIATOSŁAW\n  ════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->\n  <div id=\"tab-graf\" class=\"tab-panel\">\n  <div class=\"doc-inner\">\n\n\n    <header class=\"masthead\">\n      <div>\n        <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n          <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/graf-full.webp\" alt=\"Portrait of Graf Światosław Odrowąż\">\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Graf Światosław Odrowąż · b. 1208</div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"name-block\">\n        <div class=\"name-eyebrow\">Ghoul · Retainer · Domitor: Tomisława z Białowieży</div>\n        <h1 class=\"char-name\">Graf Światosław<br><span class=\"nickname\">Odrowąż</span></h1>\n        <p class=\"char-subtitle\">Landless Nobleman of Silesia · Head of Household (Nominally)</p>\n        <dl class=\"identity-rows\">\n          <dt>Bond</dt>       <dd>Blood Bond (3rd drink) · Ghouled upon marriage</dd>\n          <dt>Status</dt>     <dd>Known · Resources: Rich</dd>\n          <dt>Languages</dt>  <dd>Old Polish</dd>\n          <dt>Role</dt>       <dd>Public face · Military command · Memory clouding</dd>\n        </dl>\n      </div>\n    </header>\n\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">On the Subject</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"bio\">\n        <p>Nominally the head of the household. She is the neck. He was ghouled and Blood Bonded immediately upon marriage — there was no interval between husband and thrall. Tomisława uses Domitor's Favor monthly to suppress his defiance and prevent the bond from weakening. She forbids him from touching her person. This rule is absolute.</p>\n        <p>He has no occult knowledge and no conceptual framework for what has been done to him. He knows something is profoundly wrong with what he feels but cannot name it. His Composure 4 means he does not show this. The mask is so complete it may be protecting her from information she would find uncomfortable.</p>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"callout\" style=\"margin-top:1rem;\">\n        <div class=\"callout-label\">What Will Be Discovered in Play</div>\n        <p class=\"callout-body\">She will eventually want him to want her of his own volition, and will discover that his volition is permanently compromised and cannot be trusted to be fully his. The Blood Bond weakens without reinforcement. Domitor's Favor prevents that weakening. By the time she wants the answer, she will not know how many years of the real Światosław she has already erased. He performs equanimity for her. She performs untouchability for him.</p>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">Attributes</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"graf\" data-part=\"attributes\"></div>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">Skills</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"graf\" data-part=\"skills\"></div>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">Power, Merits &amp; Flaws</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"graf\" data-part=\"powers\"></div>\n      <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"graf\" data-part=\"advantages\"></div>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">Tarot Profile</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"tarot-grid\">\n        <div class=\"tarot-card\">\n          <div class=\"tarot-label\">Core Trait</div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-card-name\">Five of Pentacles</div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-desc\">Financial hardship; no land, struggle.</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"tarot-card\">\n          <div class=\"tarot-label\">Goal</div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-card-name\">Ten of Swords (Reversed)</div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-desc\">Recovery from a devastating blow — loss of inheritance and the Mongol invasion.</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"tarot-card\">\n          <div class=\"tarot-label\">Barrier</div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-card-name\">Judgment</div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-desc\">Spiritual awakening, a call to higher purpose, rebirth. Blocked by the blood bond and lack of agency.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════\n       TAB 3 — THE FOUR ALLIES\n  ════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->\n  <div id=\"tab-allies\" class=\"tab-panel\">\n  <div class=\"doc-inner\">\n\n\n    <div class=\"name-eyebrow\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.5rem;\">The Four Allies</div>\n    <h1 class=\"char-name\" style=\"font-size:1.8rem;margin-bottom:0.3rem;\">Światosław's Men</h1>\n    <p class=\"char-subtitle\">His in practice. Hers in every other sense.</p>\n\n    <!-- KUNCZE -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">The Four</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"ally-card\">\n        <div class=\"ally-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/meathead-full.webp\" alt=\"Kuncze\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Kuncze · \"Meathead\"</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"ally-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"ally-name\">Kuncze <span class=\"handle\">\"Meathead\"</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"ally-subtitle\">Muscle · Close Combat</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"kuncze\" data-part=\"stats\"></div>\n          <p class=\"ally-bio\">Pure physical muscle. Low mental and social stats across the board. Most comfortable when someone else is making the decisions.</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"kuncze\" data-part=\"advantages\"></div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-grid\" style=\"margin-top:0.85rem;\">\n            <div class=\"tarot-card\"><div class=\"tarot-label\">Core</div><div class=\"tarot-card-name\">Wheel of Fortune</div><div class=\"tarot-desc\">Fatalistic, generally lucky.</div></div>\n            <div class=\"tarot-card\"><div class=\"tarot-label\">Goal</div><div class=\"tarot-card-name\">The Hierophant</div><div class=\"tarot-desc\">Traditionalist; hopes to see the restoration of the lands of his youth.</div></div>\n            <div class=\"tarot-card\"><div class=\"tarot-label\">Barrier</div><div class=\"tarot-card-name\">Nine of Wands</div><div class=\"tarot-desc\">Resilience in adversity — always on the defensive, never able to build.</div></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- BARTUSZ -->\n      <div class=\"ally-card\">\n        <div class=\"ally-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/groom-full.webp\" alt=\"Bartusz\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Bartusz · \"Groom\"</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"ally-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"ally-name\">Bartusz <span class=\"handle\">\"Groom\"</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"ally-subtitle\">Logistics · Horses · Hospitality</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"bartusz\" data-part=\"stats\"></div>\n          <p class=\"ally-bio\">Inveterate gossip with perpetual get-rich-quick schemes who blames his drinking companions for his failures. Charming and unreliable in equal measure. Medicine 2 is a practical bonus for a traveling retinue.</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"bartusz\" data-part=\"advantages\"></div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-grid\" style=\"margin-top:0.85rem;\">\n            <div class=\"tarot-card\"><div class=\"tarot-label\">Core</div><div class=\"tarot-card-name\">Page of Swords (Rev.)</div><div class=\"tarot-desc\">Inveterate gossip; talks a big game but can't back it up.</div></div>\n            <div class=\"tarot-card\"><div class=\"tarot-label\">Goal</div><div class=\"tarot-card-name\">Ten of Pentacles (Rev.)</div><div class=\"tarot-desc\">Constant get-rich-quick schemes that never pan out.</div></div>\n            <div class=\"tarot-card\"><div class=\"tarot-label\">Barrier</div><div class=\"tarot-card-name\">Three of Cups (Rev.)</div><div class=\"tarot-desc\">Blames good company and drink for foiling his inevitable success.</div></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- ANDRZEJ -->\n      <div class=\"ally-card\">\n        <div class=\"ally-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/tracker-full.webp\" alt=\"Andrzej\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Andrzej · \"Tracker\"</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"ally-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"ally-name\">Andrzej <span class=\"handle\">\"Tracker\"</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"ally-subtitle\">Scout · Investigator · Pathfinder</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"andrzej\" data-part=\"stats\"></div>\n          <p class=\"ally-bio\">Scout and investigator. Always planning ahead, always prepared for the journey. His long-term arc is toward departure — he will eventually need to seek his own path.</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"andrzej\" data-part=\"advantages\"></div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-grid\" style=\"margin-top:0.85rem;\">\n            <div class=\"tarot-card\"><div class=\"tarot-label\">Core</div><div class=\"tarot-card-name\">Three of Wands</div><div class=\"tarot-desc\">Foresight, planning ahead. Always prepared for the journey.</div></div>\n            <div class=\"tarot-card\"><div class=\"tarot-label\">Goal</div><div class=\"tarot-card-name\">The Lovers (Rev.)</div><div class=\"tarot-desc\">Ultimately needs to break away from the retinue and seek his own path alone.</div></div>\n            <div class=\"tarot-card\"><div class=\"tarot-label\">Barrier</div><div class=\"tarot-card-name\">Five of Wands (Rev.)</div><div class=\"tarot-desc\">Avoiding confrontation.</div></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- PIERS -->\n      <div class=\"ally-card\">\n        <div class=\"ally-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/bowman-full.webp\" alt=\"Piers\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Piers · \"Bowman\"</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"ally-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"ally-name\">Piers <span class=\"handle\">\"Bowman\"</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"ally-subtitle\">Ranged Combat · Wilderness</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"piers\" data-part=\"stats\"></div>\n          <p class=\"ally-bio\">Ranged combat specialist. Wants a particular faction in a dispute back home to prevail, and believes in potent demonstrations as the mechanism. A motivation that could pull against the retinue's interests.</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"piers\" data-part=\"advantages\"></div>\n          <div class=\"tarot-grid\" style=\"margin-top:0.85rem;\">\n            <div class=\"tarot-card\"><div class=\"tarot-label\">Core</div><div class=\"tarot-card-name\">Nine of Wands</div><div class=\"tarot-desc\">Always seeing the silver lining; and there's always a cloud.</div></div>\n            <div class=\"tarot-card\"><div class=\"tarot-label\">Goal</div><div class=\"tarot-card-name\">Four of Wands (Rev.)</div><div class=\"tarot-desc\">Wants a particular side of a dispute back home to win.</div></div>\n            <div class=\"tarot-card\"><div class=\"tarot-label\">Barrier</div><div class=\"tarot-card-name\">Eight of Pentacles (Rev.)</div><div class=\"tarot-desc\">Misdirected efforts; believes potent demonstrations will make people see reason.</div></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════\n       TAB 4 — THE HERD\n  ════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->\n  <div id=\"tab-herd\" class=\"tab-panel\">\n  <div class=\"doc-inner\">\n\n\n    <div class=\"name-eyebrow\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.5rem;\">Background: Merit — Herd 3</div>\n    <h1 class=\"char-name\" style=\"font-size:1.8rem;margin-bottom:0.3rem;\">The Herd</h1>\n    <p class=\"char-subtitle\">The household that feeds her · Eight named retainers · Two Resonances available per week</p>\n\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">On the Herd</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"herd-note\">\n        <p>The vessels are the household itself — the eight named below. None of them knows what they are to her. They know about the hunt only in the abstract: periodically one is selected for a \"day's outing\" and returns with bruises, lacerations, torn clothing, and a muddled, blissed mind. They compete for their lady's favor to avoid selection, as her choice falls on those who last displeased her.</p>\n        <p>Światosław clouds their memories afterward. It requires him to touch them — the people she hunts, the people she will not let touch her.</p>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"callout\" style=\"margin-top:1.5rem;\">\n        <div class=\"callout-label\">Mechanical Note — Herd 3</div>\n        <div class=\"wop-book\" data-name=\"Herd\"></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"callout\" style=\"margin-top:0.75rem;\">\n        <div class=\"callout-label\">Social Architecture</div>\n        <p class=\"callout-body\">The herd polices itself through social anxiety. Punishment looks like being chosen. Her choice tends to fall on those who last displeased her — which incentivizes constant, visible efforts to please. They compete for her approval not to gain anything, but to avoid being selected. This is a system she did not need to design. It emerged.</p>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- ── THE HOUSEHOLD ROSTER ── -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">The Household</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"humour-intro\">The named members of the retinue who eat at her table and sleep under her roof — and who are, without knowing it, the vessels. They are arranged below by the four humours, two to each temperament, the cast of dispositions a household of this size tends to settle into.</p>\n\n      <!-- CHOLERIC -->\n      <div class=\"humour-band\"><span>Choleric</span><span class=\"humour-gloss\">hot &amp; dry · yellow bile · drive, temper, the need to command</span></div>\n\n      <div class=\"ally-card\">\n        <div class=\"ally-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/szafarz.webp\" alt=\"Przecław\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Przecław · \"Svafarz\"</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"ally-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"ally-name\">Przecław <span class=\"handle\">\"Svafarz\"</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"ally-subtitle\">Steward · Household Administration</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"przeclaw\" data-part=\"stats\"></div>\n          <p class=\"ally-bio\">He controls everything within his domain through sheer force of organization and will not tolerate slippage. The retinue runs well because he makes it run well, and he knows it.</p>\n          <div class=\"handling-note\">\n            <div class=\"handling-label\">Handling</div>\n            <p>He responds to authority he respects and ignores authority he doesn't. Undermine him in front of the servants and he will make your life quietly difficult.</p>\n            <p>Go to him with a problem, not a complaint. He wants solutions, not commiseration.</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"ally-card\">\n        <div class=\"ally-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/body-servant.webp\" alt=\"Tomasz\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Tomasz · \"Body-Servant\"</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"ally-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"ally-name\">Tomasz <span class=\"handle\">\"Body-Servant\"</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"ally-subtitle\">The Graf's Body-Servant</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"tomasz\" data-part=\"stats\"></div>\n          <p class=\"ally-bio\">He has strong opinions and no wisdom yet to govern them. He is certain he sees things others miss and is wrong about this more often than he is right.</p>\n          <div class=\"handling-note\">\n            <div class=\"handling-label\">Handling</div>\n            <p>He can be provoked into saying something indiscreet by anyone who flatters his intelligence. He thinks being listened to is the same as being respected.</p>\n            <p>He is loyal to the Graf specifically, not the household. If he perceives Światosław being diminished, he will react before he thinks.</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- SANGUINE -->\n      <div class=\"humour-band\"><span>Sanguine</span><span class=\"humour-gloss\">hot &amp; moist · blood · warmth, optimism, easy attachment</span></div>\n\n      <div class=\"ally-card\">\n        <div class=\"ally-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/messenger.webp\" alt=\"Miłosz\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Miłosz · \"Herald\"</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"ally-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"ally-name\">Miłosz <span class=\"handle\">\"Herald\"</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"ally-subtitle\">Herald · Outrider · Messenger</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"milosz\" data-part=\"stats\"></div>\n          <p class=\"ally-bio\">He genuinely believes things will work out, and they usually do, which has done nothing to temper the belief. He rides ahead with real enthusiasm and comes back with good news whenever possible — and when the news is bad, he still somehow makes it sound manageable.</p>\n          <div class=\"handling-note\">\n            <div class=\"handling-label\">Handling</div>\n            <p>He is the easiest person in the retinue to like and the hardest to take seriously. Players who need reliable intelligence should verify what he tells them.</p>\n            <p>He responds well to being given responsibility. Being trusted visibly improves his performance.</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"ally-card\">\n        <div class=\"ally-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/zofia-younger.webp\" alt=\"Elżbieta\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Elżbieta · \"Junior Zofia\"</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"ally-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"ally-name\">Elżbieta <span class=\"handle\">\"Junior Zofia\"</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"ally-subtitle\">Junior Zofia · Lady's Maid</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"elzbieta\" data-part=\"stats\"></div>\n          <p class=\"ally-bio\">She attaches quickly and completely — to people, to places, to any small kindness shown to her. She has been in the retinue long enough to have developed a quiet devotion to Bronisława and an uncomplicated admiration for the Graf.</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"elzbieta\" data-part=\"advantages\"></div>\n          <div class=\"handling-note\">\n            <div class=\"handling-label\">Handling</div>\n            <p>She is easiest to manipulate through affection. A kind word from someone she likes will get more out of her than any threat.</p>\n            <p>Her loyalty to Bronisława is genuine. Anything that threatens or demeans the senior zofia will upset Elżbieta visibly, even if she says nothing.</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- MELANCHOLIC -->\n      <div class=\"humour-band\"><span>Melancholic</span><span class=\"humour-gloss\">cold &amp; dry · black bile · endurance, grief, long memory</span></div>\n\n      <div class=\"ally-card\">\n        <div class=\"ally-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/zofia-elder.webp\" alt=\"Bronisława\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Bronisława · \"Senior Zofia\"</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"ally-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"ally-name\">Bronisława <span class=\"handle\">\"Senior Zofia\"</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"ally-subtitle\">Senior Zofia · Lady's Maid</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"bronislawa\" data-part=\"stats\"></div>\n          <p class=\"ally-bio\">Her husband was killed by the Mongols. She performs her duties with flawless composure and feels nothing while doing it.</p>\n          <div class=\"handling-note\">\n            <div class=\"handling-label\">Handling</div>\n            <p>She is unfailingly correct in her conduct and will never give anyone a reason to criticize her. Getting anything personal out of her requires real trust built over time.</p>\n            <p>She watches Tomisława with the careful attention of someone who has learned to read dangerous women.</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"ally-card\">\n        <div class=\"ally-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/wagoner.webp\" alt=\"Bogdan\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Bogdan · \"Wagoner\"</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"ally-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"ally-name\">Bogdan <span class=\"handle\">\"Wagoner\"</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"ally-subtitle\">Wagoner · Transport</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"bogdan\" data-part=\"stats\"></div>\n          <p class=\"ally-bio\">A survivor who won by outlasting things he never speaks of. He holds onto slights for years and trusts the horses more than people.</p>\n          <div class=\"handling-note\">\n            <div class=\"handling-label\">Handling</div>\n            <p>He shows resentment through slowness and silence. To get cooperation, treat his opinion like it matters — even briefly.</p>\n            <p>He volunteers nothing. Ask the right question and he'll answer; ask the wrong one and he'll stare at his hands.</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- PHLEGMATIC -->\n      <div class=\"humour-band\"><span>Phlegmatic</span><span class=\"humour-gloss\">cold &amp; moist · phlegm · calm, stubbornness, detachment</span></div>\n\n      <div class=\"ally-card\">\n        <div class=\"ally-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/cook.webp\" alt=\"Dobrawa\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Dobrawa · \"Cook\"</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"ally-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"ally-name\">Dobrawa <span class=\"handle\">\"Cook\"</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"ally-subtitle\">Cook</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"dobrawa\" data-part=\"stats\"></div>\n          <p class=\"ally-bio\">She makes bad decisions with total confidence and has the burn scars to prove it. She is entirely unbothered by consequences she should have seen coming.</p>\n          <div class=\"handling-note\">\n            <div class=\"handling-label\">Handling</div>\n            <p>She'll attempt things outside her competence without asking permission. The food is usually fine; everything else is a gamble.</p>\n            <p>She has no instinct for danger. She needs to be told explicitly when a situation is serious, or she won't register it.</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"ally-card\">\n        <div class=\"ally-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/cooks-boy.webp\" alt=\"Wojtek\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Wojtek · \"Cook's Boy\"</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"ally-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"ally-name\">Wojtek <span class=\"handle\">\"Cook's Boy\"</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"ally-subtitle\">Cook's Boy</p>\n          <div class=\"wop-sheet\" data-character=\"wojtek\" data-part=\"stats\"></div>\n          <p class=\"ally-bio\">He has learned to stay very still when adults are in conflict and wait for it to pass. For a boy his age he is unnervingly good at being unnoticed.</p>\n          <div class=\"handling-note\">\n            <div class=\"handling-label\">Handling</div>\n            <p>He sees and hears more than anyone gives him credit for. The right player might realize he's a useful pair of eyes if they bother to be kind to him.</p>\n            <p>He will not take sides. Press him and he goes blank.</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div><!-- .document -->\n", "people": "<div class=\"document\">\n  <div class=\"doc-inner\">\n\n\n    <div class=\"page-eyebrow\">Those met upon the road, the beach, and the court</div>\n    <h1 class=\"page-title\">Dramatis Personae</h1>\n    <p class=\"page-subtitle\">The people the envoy has met — and one it lied about</p>\n    <p class=\"page-lede\">Compiled from Sessions One through Four. Attributions marked <em>presumed</em> are the coterie's best reading, not established fact.</p>\n\n    <!-- THE STOWAWAY -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"cluster-label\"><span>The Stowaway</span><span class=\"cluster-gloss\">aboard the whole voyage, unseen</span></div>\n\n      <div class=\"char-card npc\">\n        <div class=\"char-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/ludan-full.webp\" alt=\"Ludan\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Ludan · \"the Red\"</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"char-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"pc-name\">Ludan <span class=\"handle\">\"a bolt of red\" · Nosferatu</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"pc-subtitle\">Stowaway · wanted by the Order of Absolution</p>\n          <div class=\"stat-row\">\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Clan <strong>Nosferatu?</strong></span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">From \"all over\"</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Masked</span>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">A lid came off a box nobody had accounted for and a man sat up in it: patchwork leathers, a wooden mask with slits for the eyes and mouth and a mustache carved into it, a shock of red hair, and a box full of dead rodents. He had ridden the whole voyage unseen. Eustace couldn't get eye contact through the mask — which ruled out Dominate and annoyed him considerably — and later worked it out on politics: masks are courtesy in courts that contain Toreador, worn by the disfigured, and an unseen passenger points one way. <strong>Nosferatu</strong> — some of the coterie take his clan as known, though Tomisława herself does not.</p>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">Going overboard from the burning ship, he yelled <em>fucking Catholics</em> at the approaching riders and followed the frenzied pair into the surf. On the beach he gave <strong>Oscar</strong> a small wooden coin, well carved with a dancing fox — <em>if you find yourself around Cambridge, ask for a bolt of red</em> — and gave <strong>Eustace</strong> a knife: <em>okay, you're forgiven.</em> Then: <em>if anyone asks, you didn't see me.</em> And he was into the woods. Oscar read him as lying through his teeth about something, and let him go anyway.</p>\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <div class=\"callout-label\">What the Order believes</div>\n            <p class=\"callout-body\">He carries something very important. Sister Mary hears the intention more clearly than the details. The coterie has his trail — Cambridge, a bolt of red — and lied to a knightly order about knowing it.</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- THE ORDER OF ABSOLUTION -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"cluster-label\"><span>The Order of Absolution</span><span class=\"cluster-gloss\">a red Maltese cross on black · seated at Norwich</span></div>\n\n      <div class=\"char-card npc\">\n        <div class=\"char-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/guarin-full.webp\" alt=\"Guarin of Jersey\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Guarin of Jersey</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"char-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"pc-name\">Guarin of Jersey <span class=\"handle\">Commander of the Order · Kindred</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"pc-subtitle\">The one she did not choose</p>\n          <div class=\"stat-row\">\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Kindred</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Lower generation</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">French heraldry · Jersey</span>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">He rode up, lifted his visor, apologised for the butchery, and produced a boarding ramp his men had been carrying for exactly this — then offered hospitality to \"her cousin and herself,\" pets and servants included, looking at the wolf's bloody muzzle and saying yes anyway. Oscar read him as an entirely sincere man, properly carried, none of Oscar's own jadedness in him — and wearing ornate ceremonial armour that has seen far more pageantry than battle. Worked into it: <strong>sigils</strong>, not runic, of no school Méabh recognises. Someone with real power made it, or gave it to him.</p>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">Starving on the barn floor, Tomisława took his freely-offered wrist and drank. His blood is noticeably thicker than her own.</p>\n          <div class=\"callout blood\">\n            <div class=\"callout-label\">The Blood Bond</div>\n            <p class=\"callout-body\">By her <em>Bond Slave</em> flaw, one drink is the whole bond. She did not run a risk; she acquired a regnant — roughly an hour after meeting him. He is a knight of an order that hunts her kind, he does not know what she is, and he does not know he holds her. She survived the barn only because he handed the one dangerous question to the nun, to whom she is <em>not</em> bound.</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"char-card npc\">\n        <div class=\"char-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/mary-full.webp\" alt=\"Mary, Sister of Sorrow\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Mary · Sister of Sorrow</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"char-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"pc-name\">Mary, Sister of Sorrow <span class=\"handle\">Nun of the Order · Salubri</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"pc-subtitle\">The healer she must keep her eyes down around</p>\n          <div class=\"stat-row\">\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Clan <strong>Salubri</strong></span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Healing by touch</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Hears intentions</span>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">A nun in full habit who knowingly walked into a vampire's frenzy to end it — pure intentions on a critical read. She stepped past the thrashing, closed her eyes, laid a hand on Tomisława's forehead, gave back three willpower and ended it. Doing it, she bled: a red stain spreading through the white at her hairline — a third eye, not a wound — which she failed to cover in time, and from which Eustace drew his conclusion. <em>The Lord speaks to me sometimes; the intention is clearer than the details.</em></p>\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <div class=\"callout-label\">The cruelty of it</div>\n            <p class=\"callout-body\">In 1242 she is a hunted remnant of the clan the Tremere are busy exterminating and libelling as infernalists — prey to the same appetite that circles the koldun, from the other direction. She is also, mechanically, the single best ally in Christendom for a five-health glass cannon with Stake Bait. And she wears a crucifix, so Tomisława cannot look directly at her. The one person who could mend her is the one person she must keep her eyes down around.</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"inst-card\">\n        <p class=\"inst-name\">The Order of Absolution</p>\n        <p class=\"inst-sub\">Knightly order &amp; Road cult — the road to Heaven</p>\n        <p class=\"inst-body\">A red Maltese cross on black. Sworn to protect pilgrims and holy sites — not crusaders — well known in the Isles and seated at Norwich, and known to be under Kindred influence, at least in Britain. They serve <strong>Theodore of Absolution</strong>, and through him the road to Heaven. Mithras is no Christian, but he is not their open enemy either. They did not come to the wreck for bandits: they were hunting a man with red hair, and possibly a mask.</p>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- THE COURT OF LONDON -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"cluster-label\"><span>The Court of London</span><span class=\"cluster-gloss\">the domain of Mithras</span></div>\n\n      <div class=\"inst-card\">\n        <p class=\"inst-name\">Mithras</p>\n        <p class=\"inst-sub\">Prince of London</p>\n        <p class=\"inst-body\">The being Tomisława chose — the first time she understood beings of his stature existed. At the debrief a door opened and the room filled with light that felt like sunlight and did not burn; the Beast recoiled on instinct, then grudgingly conceded it was in no danger. Everyone knelt without being told. There was the sound of metal meeting meat, and Thomas's head arrived in their peripheral vision. <em>Rise</em>, he said — heard the report that the envoy had revealed nothing, judged that his enemies had revealed themselves, said <em>proceed</em>, and left. The errand-boy phase is over.</p>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"inst-card\">\n        <p class=\"inst-name\">Richard de Worde</p>\n        <p class=\"inst-sub\">Nosferatu · Mithras's spymaster</p>\n        <p class=\"inst-body\">He refuses to hide his ruined face — melted, swollen flesh on open display above a nobleman's clothes, worn with evident pleasure, a standing insult to every Toreador in the court. His questions were gentle and were not gentle. He accepted Ludan as coincidence and bad luck, unconnected to the envoy's purpose. He has had eyes on Graf Światosław the entire time the coterie was abroad — and told Tomisława so, to her face, after she volunteered that her husband knew. He claimed the fault for the leak as his own before Mithras. <em>Now this means we can start the real work.</em></p>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"inst-card\">\n        <p class=\"inst-name\">Thomas</p>\n        <p class=\"inst-sub\">Mithras's man · gave them the mission</p>\n        <p class=\"inst-body\">The one who handed the envoy its errand — and, it turned out, the leak. Beheaded in front of them by Mithras at the debrief. A small number of people had been given information about the mission; it was Thomas. But <em>certain people</em> is plural, and \"the enemies revealing themselves\" is doing a great deal of quiet work in that sentence.</p>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- THE COURT OF SHREWSBURY -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"cluster-label\"><span>The Court of Shrewsbury</span><span class=\"cluster-gloss\">a border keep on the Welsh march · the first stop</span></div>\n\n      <div class=\"char-card npc\">\n        <div class=\"char-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/blackwell-full.webp\" alt=\"Ostler Blackwell\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Ostler Blackwell</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"char-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"pc-name\">Ostler Blackwell <span class=\"handle\">Earl of Shrewsbury · Kindred</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"pc-subtitle\">The honourable old warrior on the march</p>\n          <div class=\"stat-row\">\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Kindred · <strong>high clan</strong></span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Warrior-lord</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Loyal to Mithras</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Predator, not general</span>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">He has held this fort for decades against the Welsh, through mortal wars that changed hands around him — which is why de Worde called Shrewsbury the soft first stop: Blackwell is the prince's man, and the childe of <strong>Duchess Seren</strong> of Gloucester. In the hall he is a genial host and a genuinely dangerous one, a man of action who narrates his sieges while actually besting Kuncze, one of the Graf's men, and who cares, above nearly everything, for his <em>dignitas</em>. He grooms Gareth toward the Embrace with the fastidious propriety of a Ventrue, and distanced himself, unprompted, from <em>the crafting of flesh</em> the moment Tomisława named her clan.</p>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">When <strong>Eustace</strong> called out his lapse of hospitality before the whole court, the predator behind his eyes weighed turning it to blood — and he swallowed it instead, and bowed, because a sword drawn on a snide boy only makes the boy's point. He will remember it.</p>\n          <div class=\"callout\">\n            <div class=\"callout-label\">The undying stag</div>\n            <p class=\"callout-body\">His great trophy-head keeps leaving its mount and returning to the forest, angrier each time; it has cost him some of his best dogs and a houndmaster, and hangs on no wall. He has invited the coterie to a hunt for it, a night or two hence — entertainment and feast in one.</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"char-card npc\">\n        <div class=\"char-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/gareth-full.webp\" alt=\"Gareth Arundel\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Gareth Arundel</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"char-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"pc-name\">Gareth Arundel <span class=\"handle\">Lord · ghoul promised the Embrace</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"pc-subtitle\">The man who wants to be a monster</p>\n          <div class=\"stat-row\">\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Mortal · <strong>ghouled</strong></span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Tested for the Embrace</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Prominent family</span>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">The keep is nominally his family's; Blackwell rules it. A grown man, ghouled and made to wait years for a gift he plainly aches for, he had never seen a Cainite until the envoy arrived, and could not stop staring at <strong>Eustace</strong> before asking outright. On learning Eustace had been Embraced as a child — a pre-adolescent, frozen there — it was Gareth who turned cruel, needling him for it and naming his maker a bastard of a sire; Eustace, stung, could only say that to point it out was rude. Blackwell trains him in etiquette much as Tomisława trains Méabh. He is watchful, prickly, and — after the envoy forced his lord to bow — nursing a grudge.</p>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"char-card npc\">\n        <div class=\"char-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/henrietta-full.webp\" alt=\"Lady Henrietta\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Lady Henrietta</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"char-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"pc-name\">Lady Henrietta <span class=\"handle\">Gareth's mother · mortal, aware</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"pc-subtitle\">The quiet one who sees everything</p>\n          <div class=\"stat-row\">\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Mortal</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Aware of the Kindred</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Watchful</span>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">Gareth's mother, entirely aware of what surrounds her son, and content to watch the whole evening in near-silence over a cup of wine — missing nothing. When the talk turned to the abbey, she cleared her throat and told the tale of <strong>Saint Winifred</strong> properly: the maiden beheaded by her spurned suitor Caradog, the spring risen where her head fell, and her life restored by Saint Beuno, whose relics later made the town's abbey a thriving pilgrim site. She agreed to lead Tomisława — veiled — to that abbey herself, after the hunt.</p>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"char-card npc\">\n        <div class=\"char-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/dylan-full.webp\" alt=\"Dylan the Welshman\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Dylan · the Welshman</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"char-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"pc-name\">Dylan <span class=\"handle\">The Welshman · Kindred, commoner</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"pc-subtitle\">The guest who speaks to wolves</p>\n          <div class=\"stat-row\">\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Kindred</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Welsh · commoner</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Speaks to beasts</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\"><strong>Gangrel?</strong></span>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">A guest at Blackwell's court — Cainite and commoner both, \"of no account twice over,\" as Tomisława put it while teaching Méabh how to be courteous to him anyway. He crouched into a wordless conversation with <strong>Scéalán</strong>, trading small animal sounds, and warmed to <strong>Méabh</strong> as the one soul in the hall who speaks his language — telling her, cheekily, that the wolf preferred his company. He prods the lord's stories along and keeps his own counsel: cagey about his age, and about his sire, which Méabh read as the short lineage of a <strong>Gangrel</strong> without his ever confirming it.</p>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- THE ABBEY OF SHREWSBURY -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"cluster-label\"><span>The Abbey of Shrewsbury</span><span class=\"cluster-gloss\">Saint Winifred's shrine · across the river</span></div>\n\n      <div class=\"char-card npc\">\n        <div class=\"char-portrait\">\n          <div class=\"portrait-frame\">\n            <img src=\"campaign/assets/portraits/larkin-full.webp\" alt=\"Brother Larkin\">\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"portrait-caption\">Brother Larkin</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"char-name-block\">\n          <h3 class=\"pc-name\">Brother Larkin <span class=\"handle\">Monk of the abbey · Kindred</span></h3>\n          <p class=\"pc-subtitle\">The devout one</p>\n          <div class=\"stat-row\">\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Kindred</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\"><strong>Sincerely devout</strong></span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Keeps the relics</span>\n            <span class=\"stat-chip\">Mind Disciplines</span>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"char-bio\">A Cainite monk who presides over the town's abbey — not Blackwell's court — and the rarest sort of Kindred: genuinely, unshakeably faithful. He holds that God does not curse, that the Embrace is a purpose still to be discovered, and takes all comers who believe in a benevolent higher power. He reveres <strong>Mary, the Sister of Sorrows</strong> as very nearly a saint, has written even to as-yet-unredeemed <strong>Tremere</strong> in hope of their souls, and has been pressing Blackwell to let the <strong>Order of Absolution</strong> garrison the abbey and guard its pilgrims. He keeps the relics of <strong>Saint Winifred</strong> — a skull and bones which, examined by a koldun's eye, proved entirely inert: holy, but no artifact.</p>\n          <div class=\"callout blood\">\n            <div class=\"callout-label\">The night it went wrong</div>\n            <p class=\"callout-body\">He alone saw the <em>darkness</em> in Méabh and moved to correct it — and the coterie's Beast erupted. He mind-broke Tomisława into trying to take the bones, drove a stake into Oscar's back, and was compelled and pinned for his trouble. They parted on a pact of silence, and a warning from him: <em>a ghost walks his halls.</em></p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    </div>\n</div><!-- .document -->\n", "setting": "<div class=\"document\">\n  <div class=\"doc-inner\">\n\n    <div class=\"page-eyebrow\">The world the envoy moves through</div>\n    <h1 class=\"page-title\">The Setting</h1>\n    <p class=\"page-subtitle\">London and the Baronies of Avalon, 1242 — under the domain of Mithras</p>\n    <p class=\"page-lede\">A Vampire: The Dark Ages chronicle. This is <em>before</em> the Inquisition, the formation of the sects, and the Beckoning. Most danger comes from regional or political conflicts, personal strife, and greater horrors that lurk in the lands between protected domains.</p>\n\n    <!-- OUR STORY -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">Our Story</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"prose\">The year is 1242. The War of Princes engulfs Europe and beyond. The meticulous game of the Long Night has given way to an escalation of violence among the Cainites. The destruction of mighty empires has awakened a sense of desperation and paranoia among many Elders. Others seek to exploit the opportunities birthed by the chaos.</p>\n      <p class=\"prose\">As wars rage across battlefields, in palace courts, and within the shadows, our coterie receives a task to take them across the Baronies of Avalon and beyond. Their choices may tip the scales of power across Cainite society — or leave them the latest victims of the endless violence.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- LORE & LIBERTIES -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">Lore &amp; Liberties</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"prose\">The Dark Ages book is shared for reference, but its lore is not authoritative for our game. We aim for some historical accuracy while taking liberties with Vampire lore, so we are not retelling established tales but forging our own narrative. Some NPCs may be recognizable, others brand new — all molded to best serve our shared story.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- THE WORLD IN 1242 -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">The World in 1242</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"callout blood\">\n        <div class=\"callout-label\">Mithras · Prince of London</div>\n        <p class=\"callout-body\">A powerful elder who has cultivated a godlike mythology, Mithras rules the Baronies of Avalon from his seat in London. Most domains follow the Traditions of Caine; here, Mithras enforces his own <em>Blood Laws</em>, which remove worship of Caine — the Covenant — as a rule.</p>\n      </div>\n\n      <div class=\"callout\">\n        <div class=\"callout-label\">The Rose Treaty</div>\n        <p class=\"callout-body\">Recently established to end a revolt of the Baronies, recognizing their domains and granting Barons representation on Mithras's council. Great tension remains between many Baronies and Mithras; hostilities between the Tremere and Mithras are especially volatile.</p>\n      </div>\n\n      <p class=\"field-label\" style=\"margin-top:1.75rem;\">The Other Governing Powers</p>\n      <p class=\"prose\" style=\"font-size:0.97rem;\">Beyond Avalon, the night is carved between elder powers — most of them currently embroiled in some form of active conflict.</p>\n      <div class=\"powers-grid\">\n        <div class=\"inst-card\">\n          <div class=\"inst-name\">The Courts of Love</div>\n          <div class=\"inst-sub\">France</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"inst-card\">\n          <div class=\"inst-name\">The Fiefs of the Black Cross</div>\n          <div class=\"inst-sub\">the Holy Roman Empire</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"inst-card\">\n          <div class=\"inst-name\">The Sea of Shadows</div>\n          <div class=\"inst-sub\">the Iberian Peninsula</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"inst-card\">\n          <div class=\"inst-name\">The Voivodate</div>\n          <div class=\"inst-sub\">the Carpathian Mountains</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"inst-card\">\n          <div class=\"inst-name\">The Italian City States</div>\n          <div class=\"inst-sub\">the Italian peninsula</div>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"inst-card\">\n          <div class=\"inst-name\">The Ashirra</div>\n          <div class=\"inst-sub\">Arabia and North Africa</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <p class=\"field-label\" style=\"margin-top:1.75rem;\">The Customs of the Night</p>\n      <ul class=\"thread-list\">\n        <li><strong>Caine</strong> is widely accepted as the progenitor of vampires.</li>\n        <li>It is common for Kindred to practice a major religion such as <strong>Christianity or Islam</strong>.</li>\n        <li>Elder vampires exert near-absolute control over younger Cainites in most domains.</li>\n        <li><strong>Clan</strong> plays a major part in most Cainites' lives, establishing their place in society and defining their enemies. Tension and mistrust among certain clans is key to the political horror of the Dark Ages.</li>\n        <li>Most domains follow the <strong>Traditions of Caine</strong>. <em>(The Traditions and clan availability are enumerated mechanically in the character-creation house rules.)</em></li>\n      </ul>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- THE COTERIE -->\n    <section class=\"section\">\n      <div class=\"section-rule\">\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <h2 class=\"section-title\">The Coterie</h2>\n        <span class=\"section-ornament\">✦</span>\n        <span class=\"section-rule-line\"></span>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"prose\">You are a London-based coterie trusted as an <strong>envoy</strong> for Kindred of moderate status. Given the perils of travel at this time, this is a dangerous position — but one that comes with rare opportunities for those who survive.</p>\n      <p class=\"prose\">You may be in the coterie by choice, assigned for your skillset, pressured into it by an elder, or any number of reasons. While ultimately under the authority of Mithras, the coterie answers to various influential Cainites in need of its services. The work ranges from important diplomatic representation to simply delivering or receiving messages.</p>\n      <p class=\"prose\">The chronicle begins with the coterie on the way back to London after a simple task: picking up an unknown package from Norway. Loyalties will be tested and ambitions challenged, but the coterie should ultimately intend to work together toward a common goal. Tension between characters is welcome; constant inner-coterie turmoil is not — there will be plenty of antagonists more rewarding to engage with.</p>\n      <div class=\"callout\">\n        <div class=\"callout-label\">Meet them</div>\n        <p class=\"callout-body\">The four who answer to Mithras are set out in <a href=\"#coterie\" style=\"color:var(--blood);\">The Coterie</a>; the people the road has given them, in <a href=\"#people\" style=\"color:var(--blood);\">Dramatis Personae</a>.</p>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    </div>\n</div>\n"};
